  { id:'opqb-t15-645', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-vissym',
    stem:'**Monocular diplopia** can be due to:',
    options:['Paralytic squint.','Blow out fracture of the orbit.','Iridocyclitis.','Iridodialysis'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ A defective key, recorded and not disputed.** The bank keys **C, iridocyclitis**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42 prints the module's only table of this differential, *Table (1-3) Differentiation between Monocular and binocular Diplopia*, and its monocular column reads in full: **\"Lens subluxation · Iridodialysis · Incipient cataract · Astigmatism · Mucous strands in the tear film\"**. Iridocyclitis is on neither column. Option **D, iridodialysis, is on the list** — and `L25) Trauma I` prints the pairing as a slide caption in its own right, **\"Iridodialysis — (UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)\"**. The key is kept as the bank prints it. Answer **C** if this appears in a paper, and know **D** for the clinic.\n\n**The rule the question is built on.** Diplopia splits on one manoeuvre — **cover one eye**:\n\n| | **Monocular (uniocular)** | **Binocular** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **What it is** | double vision **in one eye** | double vision **when both eyes are open** |\n| **Cover the other eye** | **it remains** | **it disappears** |\n| **Where the fault is** | **in front of the retina** — the optics of that one eye | **behind the eyes** — the two visual axes do not meet |\n| **Causes** (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.42) | lens subluxation · **iridodialysis** · incipient cataract · astigmatism · mucous strands in the tear film | III, IV, VI palsy · myasthenia · muscle restriction or entrapment · thyroid ophthalmopathy · corrected anisometropia |\n\n**Why iridodialysis doubles a single eye.** The iris root is torn away from the ciliary body, leaving a second crescentic aperture beside the pupil. Light now reaches the retina through **two openings in one eye**, so one object forms two images on one retina — a *pinhole* effect in reverse. Occluding the fellow eye changes nothing, because both images are already being made inside the affected eye.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Paralytic squint** | The two eyes point in different directions, so each retina receives its own image of the object — the definition of **binocular** diplopia. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42 files \"III, IV, VI nerve palsy\" under the binocular column, and p.200 makes paralytic strabismus one of the aetiologies of an incomitant deviation. Cover either eye and the doubling stops. |\n| **Blow out fracture of the orbit** | Also binocular, by mechanical restriction rather than paralysis. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.200 gives the mechanism precisely: an **orbital floor fracture** entraps the **inferior rectus**, restricting elevation, so the eyes fail to move together. The table's binocular column names \"Muscle restriction or entrapment\". |\n| **Iridocyclitis** | The keyed option. Inflammation of iris and ciliary body produces pain, photophobia, circumcorneal injection, aqueous cells and flare, and a **small, sluggish, sometimes irregular pupil** — not a second aperture. A miotic pupil narrows the beam and, if anything, *reduces* optical doubling. It is on neither column of `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** When a patient says \"double\", the first question is not *which nerve* but **\"does it go away if you close one eye?\"**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42 lists it first among the six things to establish: *\"Is it binocular or monocular?\"* — because a monocular answer sends you to the slit lamp and the refraction, and a binocular answer sends you to the motility, the pupil and, if the onset was acute, the brain.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Monocular diplopia persists when the fellow eye is covered and comes from the optics of the affected eye — lens subluxation, iridodialysis, incipient cataract, astigmatism or tear-film mucus; the bank keys iridocyclitis, which appears on neither of the book\'s two lists.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.103 (Q645), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-646', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:'All of the following statements about **extraocular muscles** are true **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['The superiors are always intortors','They are supplied by the third, fourth and sixth cranial nerves','The inferiors are always depressors','They become stronger after resection'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The inferior oblique is an ELEVATOR, so \"the inferiors are always depressors\" is the one statement that fails.**\n\n`L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 69 prints the module's only full primary/secondary/tertiary table, and it settles three of these four options at once:\n\n| Muscle | Primary action | Secondary | Tertiary |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lateral rectus | Abduction | — | — |\n| Medial rectus | Adduction | — | — |\n| Superior rectus | **Elevation** | **Intorsion** | Adduction |\n| Inferior rectus | **Depression** | Extorsion | Adduction |\n| Superior oblique | **Intorsion** | Depression | Abduction |\n| Inferior oblique | **Extorsion** | **Elevation** | Abduction |\n\nRead the two \"inferior\" rows together: the inferior **rectus** depresses, the inferior **oblique** ELEVATES. So *the inferiors are always depressors* is false, and it is the answer.\n\n**Why the other three hold:**\n\n| Option | Why it is true |\n|---|---|\n| **The superiors are always intortors** | Both of them do intort — the superior rectus as its **secondary** action and the superior oblique as its **primary** one. This is the mirror of option C and the reason the pair is tempting: the superiors really are consistent, and the inferiors really are not. |\n| **They are supplied by the third, fourth and sixth cranial nerves** | `ophthalmology.pdf` p.15, with the mnemonic printed in red: **\" LR6 - SO4 - rest 3 \"** — \"All seven extraocular muscles are supplied by the oculomotor (III) nerve except the lateral rectus by the abducent (VI) nerve and the superior oblique by the trochlear (IV) nerve.\" |\n| **They become stronger after resection** | `L29) Sqint I` slide 34 and `L30) Squint II` slide 16 name the two operations — **recession** and **resection** — and neither deck explains them, but the direction of each is standard: **resection** excises a length of muscle and re-attaches it at the original insertion, shortening and therefore **strengthening** it; **recession** re-attaches it further back, weakening it. `L30` slide 16 assigns \"Resection–Recession\" to incomplete paralysis and muscle transposition to complete paralysis. |\n\n**The compression worth memorising.** *Recti act vertically in **AB**duction; obliques act vertically in **AD**duction.* And torsion follows the muscle's name: **superiors in**tort, **inferiors ex**tort — the very asymmetry that makes option A true and option C false.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Both superior muscles intort and all six are supplied by CN III, IV and VI, but the inferior oblique ELEVATES — so the inferiors are not always depressors.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.103 (Q646), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-647', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'A patient complains of a **sudden onset of double vision when looking to the right**. A probable diagnosis is:',
    options:['Right medial rectus paralysis','Left orbital floor fracture','Right recurrent pterygium','Right total third nerve palsy'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**⚠️ A defective key, recorded and not disputed.** The bank keys **A, right medial rectus paralysis**, for diplopia appearing **on right gaze**. The right medial rectus **adducts** the right eye, and adduction of the right eye is **LEFT** gaze. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.15 prints it flatly — *\"Medial rectus | III | **Ad**duction\"* — and the p.16 cardinal-position figure draws the right eye's medial rectus arrow pointing **in**, toward the nose. The muscles that act in right gaze are the **right lateral rectus** and the **left medial rectus**. The option most plausibly began life as *\"Right **lateral** rectus paralysis\"*, which would be exactly right. The key is kept as the bank prints it; the discrepancy is recorded here rather than argued.\n\n**The principle the question is testing.** In an incomitant (paralytic or restrictive) deviation the **angle grows in the field of action of the weak muscle**, so the diplopia is worst looking in the direction that muscle pulls. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.198 demonstrates it in the chapter's most important figure — a three-row series keyed *\"Moderate Left Esotropia in primary position → normal abduction right, normal adduction left → normal adduction right, **severely restricted abduction left**\"*. Read backwards, the rule diagnoses: **name the gaze that doubles, and you have named the muscle.**\n\n**What each option would actually produce:**\n\n| Option | The gaze it doubles in |\n|---|---|\n| **Right medial rectus paralysis** | **LEFT** gaze — the right eye fails to adduct. The keyed option, and the wrong side of the midline. |\n| **Left orbital floor fracture** | **UP** gaze, and the diplopia is **vertical**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.200 gives the mechanism: an orbital floor fracture entraps the **inferior rectus**, \"restrict[ing] the movement of the eye **upwards**\". Nothing about it is horizontal. |\n| **Right recurrent pterygium** | **RIGHT** gaze — and clinically this is the option that fits the stem. A pterygium is **nasal**, so recurrent fibrosis and symblepharon tether the globe medially and limit **abduction** of the right eye, which is right gaze *(the mechanical restriction of a recurrent pterygium is not taken from the course material; `L6) Conjunctiva` teaches the pterygium's growth, indications and recurrence but not its motility effect)*. Against it: a pterygium recurs over months, and the stem says **sudden onset**. |\n| **Right total third nerve palsy** | It limits **adduction, elevation and depression** of the right eye, so the doubling would again be worst on **LEFT** gaze — and in any case **total** implies complete ptosis, which `ophthalmology.pdf` p.200 says **\"will mask diplopia\"**: a lid that covers the cornea cannot double. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** An adult with **sudden** binocular diplopia in one horizontal direction has, until proved otherwise, a **cranial nerve palsy on that side**, and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 sets the response: investigate for **diabetes** and obtain an **MRI of the brain** to exclude raised intracranial pressure and a space-occupying lesion. A sixth nerve palsy — esotropia with limitation of abduction — is the commonest single answer to the picture the stem paints.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'In an incomitant deviation the diplopia is worst in the field of action of the weak muscle, so right-gaze diplopia points to the right lateral rectus (or left medial rectus) — not to the right medial rectus, which the bank keys.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.103 (Q647), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-648', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-vissym',
    stem:'**Uniocular diplopia** is seen in:',
    options:['Paralytic squint.','Concomitant squint.','Subluxated lens.','Posteriorly dislocated lens.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Uniocular diplopia is an optical fault inside one eye, and a partly displaced lens is the classical one.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.42, *Table (1-3)*, opens the monocular column with **\"Lens subluxation\"**. `L25) Trauma I` prints the same pairing as a slide caption — **\"Subluxation — (UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)\"** — alongside \"Iridodialysis — (UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)\".\n\n**Why a subluxated lens doubles and a dislocated one does not.** In **subluxation** the zonules give way over part of the circumference only: the lens tilts and shifts, but its **edge now crosses the pupil**. Light entering the pupil is then split between two optical paths — through the lens and around it, past the phakic edge — and each path forms its own image on the same retina. Covering the fellow eye changes nothing. In **posterior dislocation** the lens falls clear into the vitreous; the pupil is left with **one path only**, an aphakic one. The eye becomes grossly hypermetropic (about +10 D of spectacle correction) and the vision blurs, but there is only one image, so it does not double.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Paralytic squint** | **Binocular.** The two visual axes point differently, so the two retinae receive different images. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42 files III, IV and VI nerve palsy under the binocular column, and p.200–201 make paralytic strabismus a cause of incomitant deviation. Occlude either eye and it stops. |\n| **Concomitant squint** | **Binocular too, and usually not doubled at all.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.197–198 explains why: a concomitant squint of childhood is met by **suppression** of the deviating eye's image \"to overcome double vision\", which is exactly what then produces **strabismic amblyopia**. Diplopia and confusion are listed as the sequelae \"in **older** age groups\", where suppression can no longer be established. |\n| **Posteriorly dislocated lens** | One optical path, one image — see above. It reduces vision by removing the eye's second refracting surface, not by splitting the beam. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Uniocular diplopia sends you to the **slit lamp and the refraction**, never to the motility. Look for a lens edge in the pupil, a tremulous iris (iridodonesis, the sign of a loose lens), an iris root tear, early cortical lens changes, uncorrected astigmatism and a broken tear film — the five entries in `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42's monocular column, in that order.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A subluxated lens leaves its edge crossing the pupil, splitting light into two paths in one eye — the classical uniocular diplopia; a posteriorly dislocated lens blurs but does not double.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.103 (Q648), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-649', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:'**Inferior oblique** muscle is the only:',
    options:['Elevator of the abducted eye.','Elevator of the adducted eye.','Depressor of the abducted eye.','Depressor of the adducted eye.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**⚠️ A defective key, recorded and not disputed.** The bank keys **A, elevator of the abducted eye**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.15 prints the opposite in two adjacent rows of one table:\n\n| EOM | Nerve Supply | Action |\n|---|---|---|\n| Superior rectus | III | **Elevation (Mainly in abduction)** |\n| Inferior oblique | III | **elevation (Mainly in adduction)** |\n\nThe elevator of the **AB**ducted eye is the **superior rectus**; the inferior oblique is the elevator of the **AD**ducted eye — **option B**. The p.16 cardinal-position figure draws it the same way: **up-and-out = superior rectus**, **up-and-in = inferior oblique**. The key is kept as the bank prints it. Answer **A** if this appears in a paper, and know **B** for the clinic and for every cardinal-position question.\n\n**Why the eye's position changes which muscle lifts it.** A muscle turns the globe about the axis it most nearly parallels, and turning the eye in or out swings that axis into or out of line with each pull. **Abduct** the eye and the superior rectus's line of pull comes to lie along the visual axis — it becomes a pure elevator. **Adduct** the eye and the superior rectus's pull swings off-axis and its elevating power collapses, while the inferior oblique — which runs from the front and medial side of the orbit backwards to the globe — comes into line and takes over the lifting *(the mechanism is supplied, not taken from the course material; both sources give the pairing without the reason)*.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Which muscle it actually names |\n|---|---|\n| **Elevator of the abducted eye** | **Superior rectus** — the keyed option. |\n| **Elevator of the adducted eye** | **Inferior oblique** — the true answer to the stem as written. |\n| **Depressor of the abducted eye** | **Inferior rectus** — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.15, \"Depression (Mainly in abduction)\". |\n| **Depressor of the adducted eye** | **Superior oblique** — p.15, \"Depression (Mainly in adduction)\", and the same muscle `opqb-t1-21` asks for from the other direction. |\n\n**The compression.** **Recti act vertically in ABduction; obliques act vertically in ADduction.** Get that pairing the right way round and the whole six-position grid follows from it — including the reason a fourth nerve palsy's hypertropia is worst **in adduction** (`opqb-t15-680`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The inferior oblique is the elevator of the ADducted eye — recti act vertically in abduction, obliques in adduction — although the bank keys the abducted eye, which is the superior rectus.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.103 (Q649), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-650', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'Treatment of **accommodative esotropia** does **NOT** include:',
    options:['Eye patching.','Glasses.','Miotics.','Cataract surgery.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Cataract surgery treats a lens, and an accommodative esotropia has nothing wrong with its lens.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.199 gives the whole mechanism in four lines: the child has **high hypermetropia**; uncorrected, they must **accommodate constantly** to see clearly; accommodation is one leg of the **near-reflex triad — accommodation, convergence and miosis** — so excessive accommodation drags excessive **convergence** with it, and the eyes turn in. \"This condition can be corrected by prescribing **hypermetropic glasses**.\"\n\n**Why the other three all belong:**\n\n| Option | Where the course material puts it |\n|---|---|\n| **Glasses** | The treatment. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.199 and p.201 (\"Glasses can treat some or all the esotropia in hyperopic patients\"). `L29) Sqint I` slide 30 is a before-and-after pair of the same infant — esotropic without spectacles, **both eyes perfectly straight** in thick plus lenses. |\n| **Eye patching** | For the **amblyopia** that the squint causes, not for the deviation. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 lists \"patching (to correct amblyopia)\" among the treatments of strabismus and marks the bullet **Tutorial**; `L29` slide 32 is headed \"Occlusion / Treatment of amblyopia\". A child with an accommodative esotropia who has already suppressed one eye needs both glasses and a patch. |\n| **Miotics** | `L29` slide 31 is headed **\"Drugs\"** and prints exactly two words: **\"Atropine\"** and **\"Miotics\"**, immediately after the two accommodative-esotropia slides. A miotic (pilocarpine, echothiophate) acts on the ciliary muscle so the eye focuses for near **without a central accommodative effort**, which removes the accommodative drive to converge. *(The deck gives no indication, strength, route or schedule for either drug — the mechanism above is supplied, not taken from the course material.)* |\n| **Cataract surgery** | Nowhere. Neither squint deck nor `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.15 mentions it. A cataract is relevant to squint only in the opposite direction — as a **sensory** cause: p.200, \"When vision is poor in one eye for a long time this eye may deviate\", and p.202's inspection list, \"corneal opacity, **cataract**\". |\n\n**The distinction to hold on to.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.199 sets **refractive (accommodative) esotropia** against **infantile esotropia** and the treatments diverge completely: accommodative — onset **2–4 years** (`L29` slide 29 sharpens it to 2.5–5), driven by high hypermetropia, **glasses**; infantile — onset in the **first year** (`L29` slide 28: **< 6 months**), large angle, constant, cross fixation, and it \"usually **requires surgery**\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Accommodative esotropia is driven by uncorrected high hypermetropia and is treated with hypermetropic glasses, patching for the amblyopia and miotics to relieve the accommodative drive — never by cataract surgery.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.103 (Q650), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-651', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-trauma',
    stem:'**Post-traumatic diplopia** may be due to:',
    options:['Iridodonesis.','Iridodialysis.','Angle Recession Glaucoma.','Uniocular hyphemia'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A torn iris root leaves a second aperture, and two apertures in one eye make two images.**\n\n`L25) Trauma I` prints the pairing as a slide caption in its own words — **\"Iridodialysis — (UNIOCULAR DIPLOPIA)\"** — and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42, *Table (1-3)*, lists **iridodialysis** second among the causes of monocular diplopia, after lens subluxation.\n\n**The mechanism.** Blunt trauma drives the aqueous backwards and outwards, and the iris root — the thinnest part of the iris, where it inserts into the ciliary body — tears away. `opqb-t18-751` defines it exactly so: *\"Separation of the iris root from ciliary body.\"* The pupil is left **D-shaped**, and beside it sits a **second, crescentic hole** through which light reaches the retina by an unrefracted path. One object, two images, in one eye — and covering the fellow eye does not abolish it.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually is |\n|---|---|\n| **Iridodonesis** | **Tremulousness of the iris on eye movement** (`opmcq-c9-17` keys exactly that wording). It is a *sign* that the lens behind the iris has lost its zonular support, not itself a cause of doubling. It matters here because the **lens subluxation** it announces *is* a cause of uniocular diplopia (`opqb-t15-648`) — so this distractor is one step away from being right, and that is the trap. |\n| **Angle Recession Glaucoma** | A **late** consequence of the same blow: the ciliary body is split between its longitudinal and circular fibres, the angle widens abnormally, the trabecular meshwork scars, and the pressure rises **months to years later**. It presents as a silent unilateral glaucoma with field loss and cupping, not as double vision. |\n| **Uniocular hyphemia** | Blood in the anterior chamber *(printed \"hyphemia\" for hyphaema)*. It **obscures** vision — a red level behind the cornea, worse when it fills the chamber — but it darkens the image rather than duplicating it. There is no second optical path. |\n\n**⚠️ The one post-traumatic diplopia this option list leaves out is the commonest.** An **orbital blow-out fracture** entrapping the **inferior rectus** is the classical traumatic diplopia — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.200 names it under restrictive strabismus, \"leading to entrapment of the inferior rectus muscle. This will restrict the movement of the eye **upwards**\". That one is **binocular**; every option printed here is an anterior-segment injury and therefore **monocular**. Read the four options together and the question is really asking which anterior-segment injury splits the beam.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Iridodialysis tears the iris root away from the ciliary body and leaves a second aperture beside the pupil, so one eye forms two images — a monocular post-traumatic diplopia.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.103 (Q651), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-652', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'In a child with a **concomitant strabismus**:',
    options:['There is restriction of gaze in the horizontal directions.','Vision is always equal in both eyes.','A fundus examination is mandatory to rule out any retinal pathology.','Treatment is always using glasses.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**A squint can be the presenting sign of a retinoblastoma, and that is why the fundus is not optional.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.202 sets out the assessment of a case of squint, and the last line of its work-up column reads: **\"Fundus examination: optic atrophy, retinoblastoma.\"** It is the only sight- *and* life-threatening item in the whole chapter, and the mechanism by which it produces the squint is on p.200 — **sensory strabismus**: *\"When vision is poor in one eye for a long time this eye may deviate causing sensory exotropia or esotropia.\"*\n\n**⚠️ Where that instruction sits.** The whole examination sequence, this line included, is inside pages the book itself marks **\"The next two pages are from tutorial .. Skip in Final\"**. Recorded as data about the book, never as permission to skip it — the question bank asks it repeatedly.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **There is restriction of gaze in the horizontal directions** | That is the definition of the *other* half of the classification. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.198: concomitant means the angle is **\"approximately equal in all directions of gaze\"** and **\"the individual extraocular muscles are functioning normally\"**. Restriction that varies with gaze is **incomitant** — paralytic or restrictive — and p.198 warns it \"may well indicate either a serious neurologic disorder … or orbital disease or trauma\". |\n| **Vision is always equal in both eyes** | The opposite is the rule in a **unilateral** concomitant squint. p.198: in children a concomitant deviation \"often causes the secondary development of **suppression** to overcome double vision and thus leads to **strabismic amblyopia**\". Only an **alternating** squint keeps the acuities equal, because each eye takes its turn at fixing (`opqb-t15-674`). |\n| **Treatment is always using glasses** | p.201 gives glasses first, but not always and not alone: **surgical correction \"may still be necessary, particularly in those children who develop esotropia before the age of 6 months\"**, and **patching** treats the amblyopia. `L29) Sqint I` slide 28 gives infantile esotropia — large angle, constant, cross fixation — the single treatment line \"**Surgery**\". |\n\n**The order of the work-up, from `ophthalmology.pdf` p.202.** History (age at onset · unilateral or alternating · diplopia · previous treatment) → **visual acuity**, looking for amblyopia → **cycloplegic refraction** → **anterior segment**, for corneal opacity and cataract → **fundus**, for optic atrophy and retinoblastoma. Every one of the last four is a search for a *cause* of the deviation, and the last is the one that can kill.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A concomitant squint keeps the same angle in every direction of gaze with normal muscles, and every child with one needs a fundus examination because a squint can be the presenting sign of retinoblastoma.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.103 (Q652), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-653', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'One way to differentiate between **unilateral & alternating strabismus** is by:',
    options:['Using the synoptophore.','Using a Maddox rod.','Performing cover uncover test.','Comparing the BCVA of both eyes.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Unilateral against alternating is decided by the COVER–UNCOVER test, and by nothing else the book names.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.203 prints the grid, and the left column — manifest comitant squint — reads verbatim:\n\n> **Cover — uncover test** (Observe the **covered eye** after removing the cover)\n> If the eye that was covered:\n> ⇨ Move to fixate again: **Unilateral squint** (it was deviating only under the cover)\n> ⇨ Remains deviated: **Alternating squint**\n\n**⚠️ The commonest error is watching the wrong eye.** The two tests in that grid watch different eyes: the **cover test** covers the fixating eye and watches **the other**; the **cover–uncover test** watches the **covered** eye **after** the cover comes off. Getting them round the wrong way inverts every answer.\n\n**Why it works.** An **alternating** squint has two eyes that see equally well, so either will happily hold fixation. Uncover the eye that was under the cover and it simply keeps fixing — it stays where it is, and the fellow eye is now the deviating one. A **unilateral** squint has one dominant eye; uncover the non-dominant one and fixation snaps straight back to the dominant eye, so the eye you are watching **moves back out** into its deviated position.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Using the synoptophore** | It assesses **binocular sensory function** — `L29) Sqint I` slide 33 lists what it is for: \"1. Simultaneous perception 2. Fusion 3. Depth perception\". It says nothing about which eye fixes. ⚠️ Note that the instrument is **photographed on that slide and never named**: the word \"synoptophore\" appears nowhere in either squint deck or in `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.15. |\n| **Using a Maddox rod** | A **dissociating** device for measuring a **phoria** — and it is named nowhere in the course material. Measured: the string \"Maddox\" returns hits in exactly three cached files (`L29`, `L30` and `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.15) and **every hit is inside those files' own not-covered lists**. |\n| **Comparing the BCVA of both eyes** | ⚠️ **Partly true, and that is what makes it dangerous.** A long-standing unilateral squint does carry strabismic amblyopia (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.198), so the acuities usually differ, while an alternating squint usually keeps them equal — the reasoning `opqb-t15-674` and `opqb-t15-676` both run. But acuity is **inference**, not the test: a recently acquired unilateral squint has not yet produced amblyopia, and a unilateral squint on a blind eye has an acuity difference that pre-dated the deviation. The book names one manoeuvre for this question, and it is the cover–uncover. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The cover–uncover test watches the covered eye after the cover is removed: if it moves back to fixate the squint is unilateral, if it remains deviated the squint is alternating.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.103 (Q653), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-654', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-white',
    stem:'A **one-year-old child** presents with a **squint**; the examining doctor notices that the **red reflex in the squinting eye appears white**:',
    options:['This red reflex is a normal finding in such age.','The child has albinism.','Squint surgery is urgent.','This condition may be associated with low birth weight.'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**A white pupil is never normal, and one of its causes — retinopathy of prematurity — is a disease of the small, early baby.**\n\n`L33,34) The White Eye` builds the differential twice. Its signs tree ends: *Pupil → retro-pupil → **Leukocoria** → Congenital cataract, **RB**, **ROP**, Coats, PFV, Toxoplasma, Toxocara, retinal dysplasia, etc.* Its five leukocoria cards then define each, and card 1 reads: **\"Retinopathy of Prematurity — Proliferative retinopathy in preterm and LOW BIRTH WEIGHT infants with subsequent epiretinal fibrosis and tractional retinal detachment.\"** That is option D, word for word.\n\n**Why a squint and a white reflex belong in the same sentence.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.200 gives the link — **sensory strabismus**: an eye that has seen poorly for a long time deviates. So in a child the deviation may be the *complaint* while the leukocoria is the *disease*, which is why p.202's work-up ends **\"Fundus examination: optic atrophy, retinoblastoma\"** (`opqb-t15-652`, `opqb-t15-681`).\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **This red reflex is a normal finding in such age** | There is no age at which a white pupillary reflex is normal. `opqb-t9-356` tests the same list from the other side, and `opmcq-c1-35` pairs each abnormal colour with its disease — retinal detachment grey, endophthalmitis yellow, **retinoblastoma white**, total cataract absent. |\n| **The child has albinism** | Albinism gives a **pinkish** glow, and for the opposite reason: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.132 attributes \"the pinkish-white color in cases of albinism\" to a **lack of iris pigmentation**, so the choroidal vessels are seen through an unpigmented fundus. It is bilateral, and it is accompanied by nystagmus, photophobia and iris transillumination — not by a single white pupil in one squinting eye. |\n| **Squint surgery is urgent** | The urgency is diagnostic, not surgical. Operating on the alignment of an eye that may contain a **retinoblastoma** would delay the one thing that matters. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 reserves surgery for a constant deviation not corrected by glasses, and marks even that clause Tutorial. |\n| **This condition may be associated with low birth weight** | The key. ROP, from the leukocoria card above. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A **white reflex in a child is a same-week referral**, not a same-year one: the two commonest causes are a **congenital cataract**, which must be operated within weeks if the eye is ever to see, and a **retinoblastoma**, which is fatal untreated. The squint is the symptom that got the child through the door.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Leukocoria in a child is never normal; its causes include congenital cataract, retinoblastoma, Coats disease, persistent fetal vasculature and retinopathy of prematurity — the last of which occurs in preterm, low-birth-weight infants.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.103 (Q654), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-655', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'Concerning **binocular vision**:',
    options:['The right superior nasal & the left superior nasal retinae are corresponding points.','The eyes of the newborn frequently shift between alignment, convergent & divergent positions.','Binocular vision is mature by the age of 9 months.','Stereopsis results from fusion of similar retinal images.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**A newborn's eyes wander, and that is normal — binocular alignment is learnt, not born.**\n\n**⚠️ This question sits almost entirely outside the course material, and the absence was measured, not assumed.** The phrase *corresponding point* returns **zero** hits across all 29 cached lecture files and all 12 book chapter caches. *Stereops-* returns three files and **every hit is a negative** — `L29) Sqint I`'s own not-covered list (\"nor is … any stereopsis test\"), and the absence lists of `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.1 and ch.15. What the course does print is `L29` slide 33, headed **\"Orthoptic treatment / For binocular vision\"**, whose three numbered items are **\"1. Simultaneous perception 2. Fusion 3. Depth perception\"** — the three grades, under a different name for the third. Everything below is therefore supplied and tagged.\n\n**Why B is true** *(not taken from the course material)*. Ocular alignment in the first weeks of life is **intermittent and unstable**: a newborn's eyes drift in and out of alignment, and transient convergent and divergent deviations are seen in a large proportion of normal infants. Stable alignment is expected by about **3–4 months**, and a deviation still present after that is investigated rather than watched. This is exactly why `ophthalmology.pdf` p.199 dates **infantile esotropia** to \"the first year of life\" and p.201 singles out esotropia **\"before the age of 6 months\"** as the group needing surgery — a constant deviation at that age is not the physiological wandering, it is disease.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong** *(all three tagged as above)*:\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Right superior nasal & left superior nasal retinae are corresponding points** | Corresponding points are the pairs that give **one** perceived direction, and they pair **nasal retina with TEMPORAL retina**, because the two eyes view the same object from opposite sides of the nose. The right superior **nasal** retina corresponds to the left superior **temporal** retina. Same-named retinal halves belong to *different* points in space. |\n| **Binocular vision is mature by the age of 9 months** | The number is too late. Fusion and stereopsis develop rapidly between **3 and 6 months**, and stereoacuity is essentially adult by the middle of the first year — which is precisely why `ophthalmology.pdf` p.197 dates the amblyopia risk to \"before the age of **8-10 years**\" and why the earliest deviations do the most sensory damage. |\n| **Stereopsis results from fusion of similar retinal images** | It results from fusion of **slightly DISSIMILAR** images. The two eyes are ~6 cm apart, so each sees a marginally different view; the small horizontal **disparity** between the two retinal images is the raw material the cortex converts into depth. Fuse two *identical* images and you get a single flat picture — which is the whole reason \"depth perception\" is the **third** grade on `L29` slide 33 and not the second. |\n\n**The three grades, for the exam.** **1. Simultaneous perception** — both images reach consciousness at once. **2. Fusion** — the two are merged into one. **3. Stereopsis / depth perception** — the residual disparity is read as depth. They are hierarchical: no fusion without simultaneous perception, no stereopsis without fusion.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A newborn\'s eyes normally shift between aligned, convergent and divergent positions; corresponding points pair nasal with temporal retina, and stereopsis comes from fusing slightly disparate — not identical — images.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.104 (Q655), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-656', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'A **25-year-old male** presented with **right esotropia**. On examination, **limitation of abduction in the right eye**:',
    options:['The angle of squint is greater when the patient fixes with the left eye rather than the right eye.','If the right eye is covered & patient is asked to point at an object in right field, his finger will point beyond the object.','A CT or MRI of the brain is required.','Crossed Diplopia is observed.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**⚠️ A defective key, recorded and not disputed.** The picture is a **right sixth nerve palsy** — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.200 defines it in one line: *\"Sixth nerve palsy: presents as **limitation of abduction** with **esotropia**.\"* The bank keys **A**, which inverts the sign it is testing.\n\n`L30) Squint II` slide 5 is **headed** with the rule: **\"Ocular deviation (2ry angle > 1ry angle)\"**, and its picture pair proves it — the same young man photographed twice, a **small** deviation when the sound eye fixes and a **much larger** one when fixation is swapped to the paretic eye. So in this patient the angle is greater when he fixes with the **RIGHT (paretic)** eye, not with the left. Option A states the reverse. The key is kept as the bank prints it; the disagreement is recorded here rather than argued.\n\n**What the two angles are.** **Primary deviation** = the deviation measured with the **normal** eye fixing. **Secondary deviation** = the deviation of the **normal** eye when the **paretic** eye is made to fix. Making a weak muscle fix demands extra innervation, and its yoke muscle in the fellow eye receives the same excess — so the sound eye overshoots. **2ry > 1ry is the signature of a paralytic squint**, and it is exactly what a concomitant squint does not do (`opqb-t15-664`, `opqb-t15-665`).\n\n**The three unkeyed options:**\n\n| Option | Verdict |\n|---|---|\n| **Point past an object with the right eye covered** | **False as written, for a stated reason.** False projection (past-pointing) appears when the **PARETIC** eye is fixing — the brain over-estimates the eye's position from the excess innervation, and the finger goes past the target, into the field of the weak muscle. Covering the **right** eye leaves the patient fixing with the sound left eye, whose projection is normal. Cover the **left** eye and the option becomes true. `L30` slide 10 names this territory **\"False orientation\"** and gives it only three symptoms — **vertigo, nausea, uncertain gait** — never a worked example; ⚠️ the deck's own not-covered list records that **past-pointing is not mentioned**, so the mechanism above is supplied and *not taken from the course material*. |\n| **A CT or MRI of the brain is required** | **True, and arguably the best answer.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201: *\"Cases with **acute onset paralytic strabismus** should be investigated for **diabetes** and an **MRI of the brain** to exclude serious causes of raised intracranial pressure.\"* Ch.14 p.184 sharpens it for this exact patient — a compressive lesion accounts for **20 %** of sixth nerve palsies, \"especially adults **younger than 45**\", and imaging is advised \"especially in children and young adults\". A 25-year-old is in that group. |\n| **Crossed diplopia is observed** | **False, and wrong-signed.** Crossed (heteronymous) diplopia belongs to a **divergent** deviation; a **convergent** one gives **uncrossed** (homonymous) diplopia. ⚠️ `L30` slide 6 prints both words over two panels that are **drawn identically** — its cache records that the figure does not actually contrast them — so the deck names the pair and never assigns either to a deviation. The assignment above is supplied, *not taken from the course material*. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** A new esotropia with failed abduction in a young adult is a **red flag**: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.200 calls the sixth \"the most common cranial nerve to be affected with elevated intracranial pressure\", and p.201 lists space-occupying lesions among the four causes. Check the glucose, image the brain, and occlude or prism the diplopia while the six-month observation window (p.201) runs.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Esotropia with limited abduction is a sixth nerve palsy, in which the secondary angle (paretic eye fixing) exceeds the primary; an acute onset in a young adult requires diabetes screening and brain imaging.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.104 (Q656), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-657', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:'Concerning the **extra-ocular muscles**:',
    options:['The lateral rectus is supplied by the 4th cranial nerve.','Both superior & inferior recti are abductors of the globe.','The main action of the superior oblique is extorsion of the globe when in primary position.','The insertion of the superior oblique is in the posteromedial quadrant of the globe.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**⚠️ The keyed option is misstated in one word, and the word matters.** The bank keys **C**, whose claim is that the superior oblique's main action in the primary position is **extorsion**. The course material says the opposite twice:\n\n- `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 69, the module's only primary/secondary/tertiary table: **\"Superior Oblique | Intorsion | Depression | Abduction\"** — intorsion is its **primary** action.\n- `ophthalmology.pdf` p.183, the book's own box for the fourth nerve: **\"Function of SO4: abduction, internal rotation, depression\"** — *internal* rotation, i.e. intorsion.\n\nSo the keyed statement is right about *which kind* of action dominates in the primary position — **torsion, not depression** — and wrong about *which way*. The key is kept as the bank prints it. Answer **C**; remember the superior oblique **IN**torts.\n\n**Why the other three fail:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **The lateral rectus is supplied by the 4th cranial nerve** | It is supplied by the **6th**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.15 prints the mnemonic in red: **\" LR6 - SO4 - rest 3 \"**. The 4th nerve supplies the **superior oblique** — which is what makes this distractor a near-miss rather than nonsense: both muscles in the question are the two exceptions to the third nerve, and the option swaps them. |\n| **Both superior & inferior recti are abductors of the globe** | They are **AD**ductors. `L1,2` slide 69 gives both the superior rectus and the inferior rectus a tertiary action of **Adduction**. The recti, apart from the horizontal pair, pull the eye *toward* the midline as a side effect, because they originate at the orbital **apex**, which lies medial to the globe's centre. |\n| **The insertion of the superior oblique is in the posteromedial quadrant of the globe** | It inserts **posterolaterally** — in the superotemporal quadrant of the globe, behind the equator. The muscle runs forward to the **trochlea** at the superomedial orbital rim, then turns sharply **backwards and laterally**; the tendon after the pulley is what reaches the globe, so the insertion is on the far side from where the belly lies. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.15's anterior-view figure labels the trochlea and the superior oblique, and `L1,2` slide 68 draws its curved arrow, but **neither source names the insertion quadrant** — that fact is supplied and *not taken from the course material*. |\n\n**Why an oblique torts and a rectus does not, in the primary position.** The obliques approach the globe at roughly **51°** to the visual axis, so most of their pull is rotational about the antero-posterior axis and only a minority of it lifts or lowers. That is the reason the superior oblique's *main* action in the straight-ahead position is torsion, and its depressing power only emerges as the eye adducts and its line of pull swings into line *(the angle and the mechanism are supplied, not taken from the course material; both sources give the actions without the geometry)*.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The superior oblique is supplied by CN IV, inserts posterolaterally behind the equator, and in the primary position acts mainly by torsion — intorsion, though the bank\'s keyed option prints extorsion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.104 (Q657), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-658', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'In the diagnosis of strabismus, all of the following are **types of cover test EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Persistent cover test.','Alternate cover test.','Prism & cover test.','Cover uncover test.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**There is no such thing as a \"persistent cover test\". The other three all exist, and the course material carries all three.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.203 heads its section **\"4. Cover test (s)\"** — the plural in brackets is the book's own — and names two within it, the **cover test** and the **cover — uncover test**, then gives **\"5. Alternate cover test\"** its own numbered heading.\n\n| Test | What is done | What it shows |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Cover test** | Cover the **fixating** eye; watch **the other** | The uncovered eye moves to pick up fixation → a **manifest** deviation (tropia). The direction it moves is the **opposite** of where it was sitting |\n| **Cover–uncover test** | Watch the **covered** eye **after** the cover comes off | Moves back to fixate → **unilateral** squint. Remains deviated → **alternating** squint. In a phoria, moves rapidly to regain fixation |\n| **Alternate cover test** | Cover one eye **2–3 seconds**, then shift rapidly to the other — **breaking binocular fusion** | Reveals the **total** deviation, latent **+** manifest. Detects heterophoria and intermittent squint |\n| **Prism cover test** | A **prism** before one eye while an **occluder** covers the other; increase the prism until no movement remains | **Measures** the angle |\n\n**⚠️ The prism cover test is the trap, and the trap is a scoped absence.** `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.15's own absence list says *\"Krimsky test and prism cover test: ABSENT\"* — and that is true of **that chapter** and false of the module. `L29) Sqint I` photographs the whole instrument set: slide 8 a **prism bar** and a loose prism engraved \"50\"; slide 9 a prism held before one eye; and slide 10 — the deck's most informative examination picture — **a prism before one eye and a black occluder over the other at the same moment**, which is the prism cover test being performed. ⚠️ The deck **never names it**: the words \"prism cover test\" appear on no slide, and no unit of deviation is printed anywhere, which is why the prism reads simply \"50\".\n\n**Why \"persistent cover test\" is the answer.** It appears in no source in this module and describes no manoeuvre. It is built to sound like the real ones: the cover test does involve *holding* the cover, and the alternate cover test involves *sustaining* it for 2–3 seconds — but neither is called persistent. If a fourth name is offered on a paper, check it against the numbered headings on `ophthalmology.pdf` p.203, which are the only ones the book gives.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The book names three cover manoeuvres — cover, cover–uncover and alternate cover — and the lectures photograph a fourth, the prism cover test; \"persistent cover test\" does not exist.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.104 (Q658), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-659', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'**Exophoria** means:',
    options:['Latent divergent squint','Latent convergent squint','Alternating exotropia','Occurs due to excess accommodation reflex'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**The prefix is the direction and the suffix is the state — read the word and the answer falls out.**\n\n| Part | What it carries | `ophthalmology.pdf` |\n|---|---|---|\n| **exo-** | turned **outwards** = **divergent** | p.197: \"**Exotropia**: Eyes turned **outwards**. Can also be called **divergent** strabismus\" |\n| **eso-** | turned **inwards** = **convergent** | p.197: \"**Esotropia**: Eyes turned **inwards**. Can also be called **convergent** strabismus\" |\n| **-phoria** | **LATENT** — appears only when fusion is broken | p.198: \"a **latent** tendency for misalignment of both eyes that becomes manifest only if binocular vision is interrupted, such as by covering 1 eye\" |\n| **-tropia** | **MANIFEST** — present with both eyes open | p.199: \"tropia refers to a **manifest deviation present when both eyes are open (no covers)**\" |\n\nSo **exophoria = latent divergent squint**, which is option A.\n\n**How a phoria behaves.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.198: during binocular viewing both eyes are aligned perfectly; cover one and **\"that eye will drift to its position of rest\"**; remove the cover and **\"the eye will realign itself with the other eye\"**. And the sentence that keeps it in proportion: **\"A minor degree of heterophoria is normal for most individuals.\"** It becomes a disease only when it decompensates, which is what `L29) Sqint I` slide 17's symptom ladder describes — **no symptoms → muscular asthenopia → intermittent diplopia → distress in watching moving objects**.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it actually names |\n|---|---|\n| **Latent convergent squint** | **Esophoria** — the mirror term, and the one `L29` slide 18 photographs (a cover–uncover slide showing two perfectly **straight** eyes, because a phoria has nothing to show until the cover goes on). |\n| **Alternating exotropia** | A **-TROPIA**, so manifest, not latent — and \"alternating\" adds a second, unrelated axis (which eye fixes), settled by the cover–uncover test (`opqb-t15-653`). |\n| **Occurs due to excess accommodation reflex** | That is the mechanism of **accommodative ESOtropia** — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.199, uncorrected hypermetropia → constant accommodation → excessive **convergence**. Excess accommodation drives the eyes **in**, so it could never produce an **exo**-deviation. Wrong direction *and* wrong state. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Exophoria is a latent divergent deviation: exo- means outward, -phoria means it appears only when binocular fusion is interrupted by covering an eye.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.104 (Q659), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-660', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:'All the following are **extraocular muscles** of the eye **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Superior rectus','Ciliary muscle','Inferior oblique','Superior oblique'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**The ciliary muscle is INTRAocular — it lives inside the eye, in the middle coat.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.15 lists the extraocular set exactly: **\"These are seven extraocular muscles of the eye: the four recti (superior, inferior, medial, and lateral) … the two obliques (superior and inferior) … levator palpebrae superioris\"**, with the first six braced together under the label **\"control ocular movements\"** and the levator separated out as \"responsible for elevation of the upper eyelid\". Superior rectus, inferior oblique and superior oblique are all on that list. The ciliary muscle is not.\n\n**What the ciliary muscle is instead.** It is smooth muscle in the **ciliary body**, part of the **uveal tract** — the middle, vascular coat of the eye, continuous with the iris in front and the choroid behind. Its job is **accommodation**: contracting it slackens the zonules, so the lens becomes more convex and the eye focuses for near. It takes a **parasympathetic** supply carried on the third nerve, which is why the same drugs that move the pupil also move the focus. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.34's *Autonomic Nervous System of Eye* table names it directly — parasympathetic acetylcholine binds muscarinic receptors on the **\"Ciliary muscles: accommodation\"** — and its *Pupillary Dilatation* table gives the drug consequence, a parasympathetic antagonist producing **\"Cycloplegia: blurring of near vision\"** alongside the mydriasis. *(That whole page is marked Tutorial by the book.)* `opqb-t14-638` tests the same boundary from the other side: ocular motility testing detects strabismus, nystagmus and limitation of gaze — but **not** ciliary muscle paralysis, because the ciliary muscle moves nothing you can watch from outside.\n\n**Extraocular against intraocular, for the exam:**\n\n| | Muscles | Function | Tested by |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **Extraocular** | 4 recti, 2 obliques, levator | move the globe; lift the lid | ductions, versions, the six cardinal positions |\n| **Intraocular** | **ciliary muscle**, sphincter pupillae, dilator pupillae | accommodation; pupil size | near reflex, light reflex, refraction |\n\n**Why the other three are safely on the list.** All three appear on both of `ophthalmology.pdf` p.15's labelled figures and in its action grid: superior rectus (III, elevation mainly in abduction), superior oblique (IV, depression mainly in adduction), inferior oblique (III, elevation mainly in adduction).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The extraocular muscles are the four recti, the two obliques and the levator; the ciliary muscle is intraocular, belongs to the uveal tract, and drives accommodation rather than eye movement.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.104 (Q660), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-661', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:'The action of the **superior rectus** is:',
    options:['Elevation, intorsion, abduction','Elevation, intorsion, adduction','Elevation, extorsion, adduction','Elevation, extorsion, abduction'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Elevation, intorsion, adduction — in that order, and the course material prints it in that order.**\n\n`L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 69 carries the module's only full action table, and its superior rectus row is the answer verbatim:\n\n| Extraocular Muscle | Primary Action | Secondary Action | Tertiary Action |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Lateral rectus | Abduction | — | — |\n| Medial rectus | Adduction | — | — |\n| **Superior rectus** | **Elevation** | **Intorsion** | **Adduction** |\n| Inferior rectus | Depression | Extorsion | Adduction |\n| Superior oblique | Intorsion | Depression | Abduction |\n| Inferior oblique | Extorsion | Elevation | Abduction |\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.15 gives the same muscle only its primary action, qualified — **\"Superior rectus | III | Elevation (Mainly in abduction)\"** — so the secondary and tertiary actions come from the lecture, not the book.\n\n**Why the pattern is what it is.** The superior rectus arises at the orbital **apex**, which sits **medial** to the globe's centre, and runs forward and laterally to insert in front of the equator on the top of the eye. Its pull therefore has three components: it lifts the front of the eye (**elevation**), it rolls the top of the cornea toward the nose (**intorsion**), and it draws the eye medially (**adduction**). The vertical **recti** intort/extort *toward* the direction of their own name and **adduct**; the **obliques** do the opposite on both counts and **abduct**. That single sentence generates all four vertical rows of the table.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | The muscle whose pattern it describes |\n|---|---|\n| **Elevation, intorsion, abduction** | Nothing — the torsion is right for a superior muscle but recti **ad**duct, they do not abduct. Swaps the third term only. |\n| **Elevation, intorsion, adduction** | **Superior rectus.** The key. |\n| **Elevation, extorsion, adduction** | Nothing — extorsion belongs to the **inferior** muscles. Swaps the second term. |\n| **Elevation, extorsion, abduction** | **Inferior oblique** (`opqb-t15-662`) — extorsion, elevation and abduction, which is why these two questions sit on the same page and must not be answered by reflex. |\n\n**⚠️ Read the whole triplet, not the first word.** All four options open with \"Elevation\", so the question turns entirely on terms two and three. `opmcq-c1-11` asks the same grid from the opposite direction — *which muscle elevates in abduction* — and keys the superior rectus.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The superior rectus elevates, intorts and adducts — the vertical recti adduct and torts follow their own name, while the obliques abduct and tort the opposite way.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.104 (Q661), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-662', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:'The action of the **inferior oblique** is:',
    options:['Depression, extorsion, abduction','Depression, extorsion, Adduction','Elevation, extorsion, adduction','Elevation, extorsion, abduction'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Elevation, extorsion, abduction — the inferior oblique is the one \"inferior\" muscle that lifts.**\n\n`L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 69 prints the row as **\"Inferior oblique | Extorsion | Elevation | Abduction\"** — the same three actions the key names, listed in the slide's own order of priority rather than the option's. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.15 gives the clinical form: **\"Inferior oblique | III | elevation (Mainly in adduction)\"**, and the p.16 cardinal-position figure puts it at **up-and-in**.\n\n**The two rules the option list is built to break:**\n\n1. **\"Inferior\" does not mean \"depressor\".** The inferior **rectus** depresses; the inferior **oblique** ELEVATES. This is the same trap `opqb-t15-646` sets from the other side, where *\"the inferiors are always depressors\"* is the false statement.\n2. **Obliques ABduct; recti ADduct.** The obliques attach behind the equator and pull the front of the eye away from the midline, so their tertiary action is abduction — while the vertical recti, arising at the medial orbital apex, adduct.\n\n| Option | The muscle whose pattern it describes |\n|---|---|\n| **Depression, extorsion, abduction** | Nothing exactly — it takes the oblique's torsion and abduction but the wrong vertical action. The closest real muscle is the **inferior rectus** (depression, extorsion) with the wrong third term. |\n| **Depression, extorsion, Adduction** | **Inferior rectus** — `L1,2` slide 69, \"Depression | Extorsion | Adduction\". *(Capital A as printed by the bank.)* |\n| **Elevation, extorsion, adduction** | Nothing — right torsion, right vertical action, but obliques abduct. Differs from the key in the **last word only**, which is the discriminator. |\n| **Elevation, extorsion, abduction** | **Inferior oblique.** The key. |\n\n**The clinical consequence worth carrying.** Because the inferior oblique elevates **mainly in adduction**, and the superior oblique — its antagonist — depresses mainly in adduction, an eye whose **superior oblique** is paralysed shows an unopposed inferior oblique: the eye **rides up in adduction**. That is exactly the fourth nerve palsy `ophthalmology.pdf` p.183 describes as a hypertropia \"increasing in adduction\", and `L30) Squint II` slide 14's montage is built around the single frame that shows it (`opqb-t15-680`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The inferior oblique elevates, extorts and abducts, elevating mainly in adduction — the one \"inferior\" extraocular muscle that raises rather than depresses the eye.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.104 (Q662), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-663', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-pupil',
    stem:'The only extraocular muscle which does **NOT arise from the apex of the orbit** is:',
    options:['Superior rectus','Superior oblique','Inferior oblique','Inferior rectus'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The inferior oblique starts at the front of the orbital floor, not at the back of the orbit.**\n\nThe course material shows this by omission rather than by statement, and the omission is consistent across three figures:\n\n- `L1,2) Ocular Anatomy, Symptomatology & Basic Eye Examination` slide 62 draws the **annulus of Zinn** at the orbital apex and labels every structure arising there or passing through it — levator palpebrae superioris, **superior oblique**, **superior rectus**, **medial rectus**, **lateral rectus**, **inferior rectus**, optic nerve, ophthalmic artery. The **inferior oblique is not on that figure at all**.\n- `ophthalmology.pdf` p.15's lateral cut-away labels the same apical set around the **Annulus of Zinn** and draws the inferior oblique separately, forward and low.\n- `L29) Sqint I` slide 2 and `L30) Squint II` slide 2 use the same borrowed orbital illustration, and its caches record that the inferior oblique is one of the muscles **never labelled** on it.\n\n**What the inferior oblique's origin actually is** *(supplied — not taken from the course material; no cached deck or book chapter states it)*: the **orbital plate of the maxilla**, at the **antero-medial floor of the orbit**, just **lateral to the opening of the nasolacrimal groove**. From there it runs backwards, laterally and upwards, under the inferior rectus, to insert on the **posterolateral** globe near the macula.\n\n**Why the other three are apical:**\n\n| Option | Origin |\n|---|---|\n| **Superior rectus** | The **annulus of Zinn** (common tendinous ring) at the apex — labelled on `L1,2` slide 62. |\n| **Superior oblique** | The body of the **sphenoid**, above and medial to the annulus at the apex — labelled on the same figure. Its *functional* origin is the **trochlea** at the superomedial rim, which is why it behaves as a muscle pulling from the front, but its anatomical origin is still posterior. |\n| **Inferior rectus** | The **annulus of Zinn** — labelled on the same figure. |\n\n**Two consequences of the inferior oblique's odd origin.** It is the only extraocular muscle **without a tendon at its origin**, and it is the only one that does **not** pass through the annulus — so it escapes the **orbital apex syndrome**, in which a lesion at the annulus takes III, IV, VI and the ophthalmic nerve together. It also explains the action: a muscle pulling from **in front and medially** to a **posterolateral** insertion must elevate, extort and abduct (`opqb-t15-662`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'All the extraocular muscles arise at the orbital apex except the inferior oblique, which arises from the orbital plate of the maxilla at the antero-medial floor of the orbit.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.104 (Q663), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-664', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'In **concomitant squint**:',
    options:['Primary deviation > Secondary deviation','Primary deviation < Secondary deviation','Primary deviation = Secondary deviation','None of the above'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**In a concomitant squint the two angles are equal, because nothing is weak.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.198 defines the type by exactly that: the angle of misalignment is **\"approximately equal in all directions of gaze\"** and **\"The individual extraocular muscles are functioning normally,\"** the eyes simply not being directed at the same target. If every muscle works, then whichever eye is made to fix, the innervation reaching both eyes is normal — so the deviation measures the same either way. **Primary = secondary.**\n\n**The two angles, defined.** **Primary deviation** is the deviation of the squinting eye while the **normal** eye fixes. **Secondary deviation** is the deviation of the **normal** eye while the **squinting** eye is made to fix.\n\n**Why a paralytic squint breaks the equality.** `L30) Squint II` slide 5 is headed **\"Ocular deviation (2ry angle > 1ry angle)\"** and photographs the same patient twice to prove it: a small angle with the sound eye fixing, a much larger one with the paretic eye fixing. Forcing a **weak** muscle to hold fixation demands extra innervation, and its yoke muscle in the fellow eye receives the same excess and overshoots. So:\n\n| | **Comitant** | **Paralytic (incomitant)** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Angle across gaze positions** | **the same everywhere** | **largest in the weak muscle's field** |\n| **Primary vs secondary** | **equal** | **secondary > primary** |\n| **Muscles** | individually **normal** | **paralysed, paretic or restricted** |\n| **Typical age** | childhood | any; new onset in an adult is the alarming one |\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong.** *Primary > secondary* occurs in no deviation described in the course material — there is no mechanism that would make the sound eye deviate **less** than the paretic one. *Primary < secondary* is the **paralytic** pattern, and the question asks about the concomitant one — the two options are a deliberate pair with `opqb-t15-665`. *None of the above* is only defensible if C were false, and C is the definition.\n\n**⚠️ A note on the option list.** These four are a bare ordinal ladder (>, <, =, none). That shape matches dozens of questions in this corpus and carries no diagnostic weight of its own — read the **stem**, which is where \"concomitant\" does all the work.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A concomitant squint has individually normal muscles and the same angle in every gaze position, so the primary and secondary deviations are equal — unlike a paralytic squint, where the secondary exceeds the primary.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.105 (Q664), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-665', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'In **paralytic squint**, the difference between the primary and secondary deviation, **in the direction of gaze of the paralysed muscle**:',
    options:['Increases','Decreases','Remains the same'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**Look toward the weak muscle and everything about a paralytic squint gets bigger — including the gap between the two angles.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.198 demonstrates the underlying rule in the chapter's most important figure, a three-row photographic series with its own key column: **\"Moderate Left Esotropia in primary position\"** → **\"Normal abduction of right eye, normal adduction of left eye\"** → **\"Normal adduction of right eye, severely restricted abduction of left eye.\"** Read together, the rows say: **the angle grows in the field of action of the underacting muscle.** `L30) Squint II` slide 5 supplies the other half, in its heading: **\"Ocular deviation (2ry angle > 1ry angle)\"**.\n\n**Put the two together.** In the paralysed muscle's own field of gaze, the muscle is being asked to do the most work it can be asked to do, so the innervational effort — and therefore the overshoot of the yoke muscle in the fellow eye — is maximal. The secondary angle rises steeply; the primary angle rises far less. So the **difference between them widens**. Turn the gaze the other way, into the field of the healthy antagonist, and both angles shrink towards each other.\n\n**⚠️ This question prints only THREE options** — A, B and C, with no D on the page. Recorded as printed.\n\n**Why the other two are wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Decreases** | This is what happens in the **opposite** direction of gaze — away from the paralysed muscle, where the deviation is smallest and the two angles converge. Right relationship, wrong gaze. |\n| **Remains the same** | An angle that does not change with gaze **is the definition of a comitant squint** (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.198, \"approximately equal in all directions of gaze\"), and a paralytic squint is by definition incomitant. Choosing this collapses the whole distinction the chapter is built on. |\n\n**Why it matters at the bedside.** These two facts together are how a paralytic squint is recognised **without any instrument**: cover-test the patient in the primary position, then again in each direction of gaze, and swap the fixing eye. An angle that grows in one direction and a secondary deviation that exceeds the primary mean **incomitance**, and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 then sets the response — investigate for **diabetes**, image the brain, and allow **six months** for spontaneous resolution before considering surgery.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'In a paralytic squint the deviation is largest in the field of action of the weak muscle, so the excess of the secondary angle over the primary increases in that direction of gaze.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.105 (Q665), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-666', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'The best treatment for **amblyopia** is:',
    options:['Orthoptic exercises','Occlusion','Surgery','Best treat after age 10 years'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Patch the good eye and force the lazy one to work. Everything else in this list treats something other than amblyopia.**\n\n`L29) Sqint I` slide 32 is headed in two lines — **\"Occlusion / Treatment of amblyopia\"** — and photographs two forms of it: an opaque black fabric occluder fitted over one spectacle lens, and a pale translucent frosted occluder stuck to the glass. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 names the same treatment as **\"patching (to correct amblyopia)\"**, and marks that whole bullet **Tutorial** — the book's tag for \"study in round, skip in final\". Recorded as data about the book; the question bank asks it anyway.\n\n**⚠️ Neither source gives a regimen.** No hours per day, no ratio, no follow-up interval, no age limit, and — the omission that matters most — **neither states that it is the SOUND eye that is covered.** *(Supplied, not taken from the course material: patch the better eye for roughly **2–6 hours a day**, graded to the severity and the child's age, reviewed about weekly per year of age, because the patched good eye can itself become amblyopic. Where patching fails or is refused, **atropine penalisation** of the good eye is the alternative.)*\n\n**The order that makes patching work.** Occlusion is the **third** step, not the first: remove any obstacle (operate a congenital cataract, lift a dense ptosis) → correct the refractive error in full, under **cycloplegia**, and wait, because some amblyopia resolves on glasses alone → **then** patch. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 states the aim in one sentence: \"the most effective way to support fusion (binocular vision) is to **treat the amblyopia and equalize the vision**\". `opqb-t15-677` is the same reasoning applied to a case.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Orthoptic exercises** | They train **binocular function**, not acuity. `L29` slide 33, \"Orthoptic treatment / For binocular vision\", lists what they aim at: simultaneous perception, fusion, depth perception. An amblyopic eye has nothing to fuse with until its acuity improves. |\n| **Surgery** | Surgery straightens the eyes; it does not make an amblyopic eye see. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 keeps them apart: glasses and patching treat the vision, and surgical correction is for a constant deviation not corrected by glasses, or for the cosmetic burden of a disfiguring squint. |\n| **Best treat after age 10 years** | The exact reverse of the rule. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.197 dates the damage to **\"before the age of 8-10 years\"**, which is also the window in which it can be reversed. `opqb-t15-670` is that fact as a clinical scenario — a 40-year-old amblyope in whom treatment has nothing left to work on. |\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Amblyopia is treated by occlusion — patching the sound eye to force use of the amblyopic one — after any obstacle is removed and the refractive error fully corrected, and only within the 8–10 year window.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.105 (Q666), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-667', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'When asked to **look to the left side**, the patient **could not move his right eye**. What is the diagnosis?',
    options:['Right trochlear palsy','Right oculomotor palsy','Right abducent palsy','None of the above'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Name the gaze that fails and you have named the muscle; name the muscle and you have named the nerve.**\n\nLooking to the **left** requires the **right eye to ADDUCT**. Adduction is the **medial rectus**, and `ophthalmology.pdf` p.15 gives its nerve without qualification: **\"Medial rectus | III | Adduction\"** — the **oculomotor** nerve. A right eye that will not move on left gaze has therefore lost its right medial rectus, and that is a **right third nerve palsy**.\n\n`L30) Squint II` slide 11 photographs the identical finding. Its six-frame montage of an elderly patient with a complete right-of-photograph ptosis includes a top-left frame in which the fellow eye abducts fully while the affected eye **has not followed** — its cache records it as **\"FAILURE OF ADDUCTION\"**, with the top-centre frame showing that **abduction on that side is intact**, because the lateral rectus is not a third-nerve muscle.\n\n**⚠️ The transcription note.** The bank prints the stem as *\"he couldn't not move his right eye\"* — a doubled negative. Repaired here to \"could not move\"; the printed form is recorded in the staging file.\n\n**What else a complete third nerve palsy shows** (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.182, p.200):\n\n| Sign | Why |\n|---|---|\n| **PTOSIS** — and it **masks the diplopia** | CN III supplies levator palpebrae superioris |\n| The eye sits **\"down and out\"** | Only the two non-III muscles still pull: **lateral rectus (VI)** out, **superior oblique (IV)** down |\n| **Loss of elevation and adduction** | Superior rectus, inferior rectus, inferior oblique and medial rectus are all III |\n| **MYDRIASIS**, in compressive lesions | CN III carries the parasympathetic supply to the sphincter pupillae — and a dilated pupil raises the question of an **aneurysm in the circle of Willis** |\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | The gaze it would fail in |\n|---|---|\n| **Right trochlear palsy** | The superior oblique **depresses in adduction** and intorts. Its palsy gives a **hypertropia** worst in adduction, **vertical** diplopia and a compensatory **head tilt** (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.183) — not a failure of horizontal movement. |\n| **Right abducent palsy** | The lateral rectus **abducts**, so a right sixth nerve palsy fails on **RIGHT** gaze and produces an **esotropia** (p.200). This is the mirror-image answer, and the reason the stem's direction has to be read carefully. |\n| **None of the above** | Only defensible if no option fitted; B fits exactly. |\n\n**⚠️ A note on the figure.** The clinical photograph printed with this question shows a grossly abnormal eye on the **image-right**, which under the usual clinical-photograph convention is the patient's **LEFT** eye — the opposite side from the one the stem and the key describe. The stem states the finding in words and is fully answerable without the picture; the discrepancy is recorded and not resolved.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Left gaze demands adduction of the right eye by the right medial rectus, a CN III muscle — so a right eye that cannot adduct on left gaze indicates a right oculomotor palsy.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.105 (Q667), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-668', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'What is the **name of the test** shown, and **what does it demonstrate**?',
    options:['Cover/uncover test left esophoria','Cover/uncover test right esophoria','Cover test left esotropia','Cover test left esophoria'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**Two panels: the cover goes on and STAYS on, and it is the OTHER eye that moves. That is the cover test, and a manifest deviation.**\n\nThe figure is a two-panel line drawing. Panel 1 shows a pair of eyes apparently straight. An arrow leads to panel 2, in which an occluder paddle covers the eye on the image-left, and the **uncovered** eye on the image-right carries an arrow labelled *\"Eye moves\"* pointing **outward**.\n\n**Step 1 — which test.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.203 separates the two manoeuvres by *which eye you watch*:\n\n| | **Cover test** | **Cover–uncover test** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Do** | Cover the **fixating** eye | Cover an eye, then **remove** the cover |\n| **Watch** | **the OTHER (uncovered) eye** | **the COVERED eye, after the cover comes off** |\n| **Answers** | is there a **manifest** deviation, and which way | **unilateral or alternating**; and in a phoria, the recovery |\n\nThe cover here never comes off and the movement is in the **uncovered** eye — so this is the **cover test**, which rules out both \"cover/uncover\" options at a stroke.\n\n**Step 2 — which deviation.** The book gives the reading rule with a worked example: *\"The uncovered eye moves to pick up fixation … e.g., **If the eye moves inward to fixate, then it was deviated outward and is exotropic**.\"* The eye **moves AGAINST the direction it was sitting**. Here it moves **outward** to take up fixation, so it had been sitting **inward** — an **esotropia**.\n\n**Step 3 — a tropia, not a phoria.** A deviation revealed by the **cover test** is manifest by definition. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.199: *\"tropia refers to a manifest deviation present when both eyes are open (no covers)\"*; p.198: a phoria \"becomes manifest **only** if binocular vision is interrupted\", and in a phoria the book's own right-hand column says the uncovered eye shows **\"no movement\"** on the cover test. Movement in the uncovered eye is therefore incompatible with a phoria.\n\nCover test + moves outward to fixate + manifest = **cover test, left esotropia**.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Which step it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Cover/uncover test left esophoria** | Wrong test *and* wrong state — the cover is never removed, and the moving eye is the uncovered one. |\n| **Cover/uncover test right esophoria** | The same, plus the wrong side. |\n| **Cover test left esophoria** | Right test, wrong state: a phoria gives **no movement** of the uncovered eye on the cover test. This is the closest distractor and the one worth understanding. |\n\n**⚠️ This question and `opqb-t15-669` are a deliberate pair** printed one above the other with a near-identical stem. The discriminators are the figures — two panels against three, cover left against cover right, arrow outward against arrow inward — and one line of the option list.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A cover test in which the uncovered eye moves outward to take up fixation reveals a manifest deviation that had been inward — a left esotropia; the eye always moves opposite to where it was sitting.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.105 (Q668), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-669', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'What is the **name of the test** shown, and **what does it demonstrate**?',
    options:['Cover/uncover test left esophoria','Cover/uncover test left exophoria','Cover test left esotropia','Cover test left esophoria'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Three panels: the cover goes on and then COMES OFF, and the eye that was covered moves back. That is the cover–uncover test, and a latent deviation.**\n\nThe figure runs in three stages. Panel 1: a pair of eyes, both straight. Panel 2: an occluder paddle over the eye on the image-right. Panel 3: the cover has been removed, and that same eye carries an arrow labelled *\"Eye moves\"* pointing **inward**.\n\n**Step 1 — which test.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.203: the cover–uncover test is defined as *\"Observe the **covered eye** after removing the cover\"*. The cover comes off, and the eye you watch is the one that was under it. Both \"cover test\" options are out.\n\n**Step 2 — phoria or tropia.** The book's right-hand column — the heterophoria column — reads: *\"If the eye that was covered **move rapidly to regain fixation** then patient has **heterophoria** (latent squint or tendency of the eye to deviate that is corrected by the brain to achieve fusion).\"* That is precisely what panel 3 draws. p.198 supplies the mechanism: under the cover, the eye \"will **drift to its position of rest**\"; once the cover is removed, \"the eye will **realign itself** with the other eye\". Straight in panel 1, drifted in panel 2, recovering in panel 3 — a **phoria**.\n\n**Step 3 — which direction.** The recovery movement is the **reverse** of the drift. The eye moves **INWARD** to regain fixation, so under the cover it had drifted **OUTWARD** — a **divergent** latent deviation, i.e. **EXOphoria** (`opqb-t15-659`: exo = outward = divergent; -phoria = latent).\n\nCover–uncover + covered eye recovers + drifted outward = **cover/uncover test, left exophoria**.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Which step it fails |\n|---|---|\n| **Cover/uncover test left esophoria** | Right test, **wrong direction** — an esophoria drifts **in** under the cover and recovers by moving **out**. The arrow in panel 3 points inward. This is the single discriminating line between this question and `opqb-t15-668`. |\n| **Cover test left esotropia** | Wrong test — that is `opqb-t15-668`'s answer, two panels with the cover left in place. |\n| **Cover test left esophoria** | Wrong test, and internally impossible: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.203's heterophoria column says the cover test shows **\"no movement\"** in a phoria, so a phoria could never be *named* by a cover test. |\n\n**⚠️ Figure-only teaching, in the book as well as in the bank.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.203's own cover–uncover cell carries red arrows showing the direction of the recovery movement and which eye makes it, and the chapter cache records that **neither is stated in words anywhere on the page**. A student who reads only the prose cannot answer this question; the picture is the teaching.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A cover–uncover test in which the covered eye moves inward to regain fixation shows it had drifted outward under the cover — a latent divergent deviation, or exophoria.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.105 (Q669), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-670', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'A **40-year-old man** is seen for an annual checkup without any complaint. He gives a history of having his **left eye diagnosed as a lazy eye since childhood**. He does not wear glasses. Visual acuity is **6/6 in the right eye and 6/36 in the left, with no improvement with a pin hole**. He is **emmetropic in the right eye**, while the **left eye has hyperopic astigmatism**. The vision did not improve with glasses in the left eye. No other abnormality is detected. What is your next step?',
    options:['Intermittent occlusion of the right eye.','Intermittent occlusion of the left eye.','Hard contact lens for the left eye.','Leave him alone'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"This patient with long-standing amblyopia (lazy eye)\n\nVA 6/36 not improving with pinhole or glasses → true amblyopia\n\n**Age 40 → amblyopia treatment no longer effective**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim from the key page. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The window, and why 40 is outside it.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.197 gives the only date the chapter contains: amblyopia develops **\"before the age of 8-10 years\"**. That same period is the one in which the visual cortex is still plastic enough for occlusion to reverse it. Beyond it, the deprivation is fixed: patching a 40-year-old's good eye costs him his working vision for hours a day and returns nothing.\n\n**Why the three negative tests in the stem matter.** Each one closes off a treatable alternative before the diagnosis of amblyopia is allowed to stand:\n\n| The finding | What it excludes |\n|---|---|\n| **No improvement with the pinhole** | An uncorrected refractive error — a pinhole cuts out the peripheral rays and would sharpen the image if optics were the problem |\n| **No improvement with glasses** | The same, tested properly with the hyperopic astigmatic correction in place |\n| **\"No other abnormality detected\"** | Organic disease — cataract, corneal opacity, macular or optic nerve pathology. Amblyopia is reduced acuity in a **structurally normal** eye *(that definition is supplied — `ophthalmology.pdf` names amblyopia five times and defines it nowhere)* |\n\n**What produced it in the first place.** The stem hands over the aetiology: emmetropia on the right, **hyperopic astigmatism on the left** — unequal refractive error between the two eyes, i.e. **anisometropic amblyopia**. It is the type that gets missed, because there is **no squint, no white pupil and no complaint** — the good eye carries the child and nobody looks. *(The classification of amblyopia into strabismic, anisometropic, ametropic and stimulus-deprivation is not in the course material; only \"strabismic amblyopia\" is named, on `ophthalmology.pdf` p.198.)*\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Intermittent occlusion of the right eye** | The correct **eye** — occlusion always patches the sound eye (`opqb-t15-677`) — but three decades too late, and it would leave a working man functionally blind while it was on. |\n| **Intermittent occlusion of the left eye** | The wrong eye as well as the wrong age. Patching the amblyopic eye is the exact inverse of the treatment. |\n| **Hard contact lens for the left eye** | A rigid lens neutralises corneal astigmatism, so it is a real answer to *irregular* astigmatism — but the stem has already recorded that **spectacles did not help**, so the fault is not optical. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line, and what \"leave him alone\" actually means.** He needs no treatment for the amblyopia, but he does need **counselling about his one good eye**: a monocular patient's right eye is now his only eye. Polycarbonate protective spectacles for sport and workshop use, and a low threshold for reviewing any symptom in that eye, are what the consultation is for.",
    objective:'Amblyopia can only be reversed within the 8–10 year visual-development window, so an adult amblyope whose vision does not improve with pinhole or glasses and who has no organic disease needs no treatment.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.106 (Q670), key + explanation p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-671', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'A **3-year-old child** presents with a **convergent squint of one year\'s duration**. The **first step in management** is:',
    options:['Proper refraction.','Surgical correction.','Training on the synoptophore.','Prism prescription.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**A convergent squint appearing at two in a child who has never been refracted is an accommodative esotropia until glasses prove otherwise.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.199 gives the entity and the ages: refractive (accommodative) esotropia \"occurs in **children with high hypermetropic** refractive error and usually manifests at **age 2-4 years**\" — this child's squint began at **two**. Uncorrected hypermetropia forces constant accommodation; accommodation is part of the **near-reflex triad (accommodation, convergence, miosis)**; the excess convergence turns the eyes in. And the treatment follows from the mechanism: **\"This condition can be corrected by prescribing hypermetropic glasses.\"** `L29) Sqint I` slide 30 proves it with a picture pair — the same infant esotropic without spectacles, and **both eyes dead straight** in thick plus lenses, with nothing else done.\n\n**Why refraction comes first even if it turns out not to be accommodative.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.202's assessment sequence puts **\"Refraction (cycloplegic)\"** second, immediately after visual acuity and before the anterior segment and fundus. You cannot measure the true angle, decide on surgery, or begin treating the amblyopia until the refractive error is known and corrected — `opqb-t15-673` makes the same point as a step in a case, listing *re-measurement of the angle after correction* among the things that are indicated. ⚠️ The refraction must be **cycloplegic**: a hypermetropic child's own accommodation hides most of the error from a dry retinoscopy.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Surgical correction** | Premature by two steps. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 reserves surgery for the child with esotropia **before the age of 6 months** (the infantile form) and, in a tutorial-marked box, for a **\"constant deviation … not corrected by glasses\"** — a condition that cannot be tested until the glasses have been tried. Operating on an accommodative esotropia that glasses would have straightened over-corrects it. |\n| **Training on the synoptophore** | Orthoptic treatment addresses **binocular function** — `L29` slide 33 lists its three aims as simultaneous perception, fusion and depth perception — and it can only build on a clear, corrected, equal-acuity pair of eyes. ⚠️ The instrument is **photographed on that slide and never named**; the word \"synoptophore\" appears on no slide of either squint deck. |\n| **Prism prescription** | `L29` slide 19 lists prisms — \"Relieving - Exercising\" — under the treatment of **latent** squint, and slide 26 gives \"base in prisms\" for intermittent exotropia. This child has a **manifest convergent** deviation of a year's standing; a prism would relieve diplopia in an adult, but this child is suppressing, not doubling. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Cycloplegic refraction → full hypermetropic correction → re-assess the angle in the glasses → treat any residual amblyopia by patching → and only then consider surgery for whatever deviation the glasses leave behind.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The first step in a young child\'s convergent squint is full cycloplegic refraction, because accommodative esotropia manifests at 2–4 years and is corrected by hypermetropic glasses alone.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.106 (Q671), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-672', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-vissym',
    stem:'The discrimination between **uniocular and binocular diplopia** depends upon:',
    options:['The performance of the cover test.','The examination of the ocular motility.','The examination with the Hess screen.','The examination with the synoptophore.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"Distinguishing uniocular & binocular diplopia\n\n**Uniocular diplopia** persists when closing the other eye → **refractive cause**.\n\n**Binocular diplopia** disappears when one eye is closed → **squint**.\n\n**Test requires covering one eye.**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim from the key page. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The box is `ophthalmology.pdf` p.42's Table (1-3) in three lines.** The book's definitions read: binocular diplopia is \"Double vision when **both eyes are open**\" and \"**disappears** when any of the eyes are occluded\"; monocular diplopia is \"Double vision **in one eye**\" that \"**remains even after occlusion** of the other eye\". Covering one eye is the entire test, and it needs no instrument.\n\n**Why the question is really about triage.** p.42 lists six things to establish in any case of diplopia, and this one is **first**: *\"1. Is it binocular or monocular?\"* — because the answer sends the patient in one of two completely different directions:\n\n| | **Monocular** | **Binocular** |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Fault lies** | in the **optics of one eye** | in the **alignment of the two eyes** |\n| **Causes** | lens subluxation · iridodialysis · incipient cataract · astigmatism · mucous strands in the tear film | III, IV, VI palsy · myasthenia · muscle restriction or entrapment · thyroid ophthalmopathy · corrected anisometropia |\n| **Next examination** | slit lamp, refraction, pinhole | ocular motility, cover test, pupil — and imaging if acute |\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **The examination of the ocular motility** | The **second** step, and only for the binocular branch. Motility tells you *which muscle* is weak once you already know the two eyes are misaligned; it says nothing about a lens edge in the pupil. `opqb-t14-622` keys ocular motility as the diagnostic test for a **diabetic** with diplopia — that patient's diplopia has already been established as binocular. |\n| **The examination with the Hess screen** | A method of **charting** a binocular deviation across the field of gaze — so again it presupposes the answer. ⚠️ And it is taught nowhere in this module: measured, \"Hess\" appears in the caches only inside `L30) Squint II`'s own not-covered list (\"THE HESS CHART IS NOT MENTIONED AND NOT SHOWN\") and `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.15's absence list. |\n| **The examination with the synoptophore** | An instrument for **binocular sensory** function — `L29) Sqint I` slide 33: simultaneous perception, fusion, depth perception. It requires both eyes open, which is the exact opposite of the manoeuvre the box describes. |\n\n**Bedside form of the test.** Ask the patient to cover one eye, then the other. If either cover abolishes the doubling, it is **binocular**. If one eye still sees double with the fellow eye shut, it is **monocular in that eye** — and a **pinhole** held before it will usually collapse the second image, which both confirms the optical origin and points at the refraction.",
    objective:'Covering one eye is the whole discrimination: diplopia that disappears when either eye is occluded is binocular and comes from misalignment, while diplopia persisting in one eye alone is monocular and optical.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.106 (Q672), key + explanation p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-673', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'A **schoolboy of 6** presented with a **convergent squint**. On examination the visual acuity was **6/18 in the right eye and 6/12 in the left**. The **angle of deviation was 25 degrees**. All of the following are indicated **EXCEPT**:',
    options:['Testing the extraocular muscle movements.','Fundus examination and refraction under cyclopigic.','Full correction of the refractive error.','Re-measure the angle of deviation after correction.','Occlusion of the right eye.'],
    answer:4,
    explanation:"**Occlusion patches the BETTER eye, and in this boy the better eye is the LEFT.**\n\nRead the acuities: **6/18 right, 6/12 left** — the **right** eye is the worse of the two. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 states the aim of treatment as **\"treat the amblyopia and equalize the vision\"**, and the way occlusion does that is by covering the eye that is winning, so the weaker eye is forced to work. Occluding the **right** eye here would patch the amblyopic eye and leave the dominant one to carry on unchallenged — the treatment upside down. `opqb-t15-677` is the same decision made correctly on a different child, and its printed box states the rule outright.\n\n**Why the other four are all indicated** — they are `ophthalmology.pdf` p.202's assessment sequence, in order:\n\n| Option | Where it sits in the work-up |\n|---|---|\n| **Testing the extraocular muscle movements** | The book delegates EOM testing to the examination chapter (\"Go to Examination chapter (Page 16)\"), where it is done as **ductions** — each eye separately, each muscle in its primary field — and **versions**. It answers the first question about any squint: **comitant or incomitant?** An angle equal in all gaze positions is comitant (p.198); one that grows in a particular direction is not, and that changes the whole work-up. |\n| **Fundus examination and refraction under cycloplegic** | Both are printed in p.202's own column: **\"Refraction (cycloplegic)\"** and **\"Fundus examination: optic atrophy, retinoblastoma\"**. The cycloplegia is not optional in a hypermetropic child — his own accommodation would hide most of the error. |\n| **Full correction of the refractive error** | p.199: an accommodative esotropia is driven by uncorrected hypermetropia and \"can be corrected by prescribing hypermetropic glasses\"; p.201: \"Glasses can treat **some or all** the esotropia in hyperopic patients.\" The correction must be **full** — a partial one leaves residual accommodative drive. |\n| **Re-measure the angle of deviation after correction** | The step that decides everything downstream. If the 25° disappears in glasses, the squint was **fully accommodative** and nothing further is needed (`opqb-t15-675`). If part of it remains, that residue is the **surgical** angle — p.201's tutorial-marked box: surgery for a \"constant deviation … **not corrected by glasses**\". |\n\n**⚠️ Two transcription notes.** The bank prints option B as **\"cyclopigic\"** for *cycloplegic*. And this question prints **five** options, A–E, where most of the topic prints four.\n\n**⚠️ A note on the numbers.** The angle is given in **degrees**, which is how this course measures — `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.15 contains **no prism dioptre anywhere**, and its only angular scale is the Hirschberg figure on p.202, calibrated 0°, 15°, 30°, 45°. `L29) Sqint I` photographs a prism engraved \"50\" and never prints a unit at all.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'The work-up of a child\'s squint is motility, cycloplegic refraction and fundus, full correction, then re-measurement of the angle; occlusion is added for amblyopia and always covers the better-seeing eye.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.106 (Q673), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-674', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'In the **6-year-old schoolboy with a convergent squint** described in the previous question — **6/18 right, 6/12 left, angle 25 degrees** — the **cover test reveals that he can fix with either eye**. The correct diagnosis is:',
    options:['Right concomitant convergent squint.','Alternating concomitant convergent squint.','Left concomitant convergent squint.','Right amblyopia.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Being able to fix with either eye is the definition of an alternating squint.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.203's cover–uncover row makes the distinction the whole diagnosis: watch the **covered** eye after the cover is removed — *\"Move to fixate again: **Unilateral squint**\"*, *\"Remains deviated: **Alternating squint**.\"* A child who can hold fixation with **either** eye has two eyes good enough to fix, so neither is condemned to permanent deviation; the squint alternates between them.\n\n**The acuities corroborate it.** 6/18 and 6/12 are close, and both are usable. That is what an alternating squint looks like sensorially: because each eye takes its turn at fixing, **neither is continuously suppressed**, so deep strabismic amblyopia never develops. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.198 gives the opposite mechanism for the unilateral case — one eye is suppressed \"to overcome double vision and thus leads to **strabismic amblyopia**\" — and `opqb-t15-676` is that case, with 6/60 against 6/6.\n\n**And it is concomitant.** The stem gives one angle, 25°, with no mention of limitation in any direction; p.198 defines concomitant as an angle \"approximately equal in all directions of gaze\" with individually normal muscles, and adds the reassurance that a concomitant deviation \"in patients **under age 6** is rarely caused by serious neurologic disease\".\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Right concomitant convergent squint** | A *right* squint means the right eye is the one that always deviates and the left always fixes — i.e. **unilateral**. The cover test has just shown he can fix with either. |\n| **Left concomitant convergent squint** | The same objection, mirrored. |\n| **Right amblyopia** | Not the diagnosis being asked for, and not established by the finding given. The right eye is the weaker at 6/18 against 6/12, but a one-line difference in an uncorrected 6-year-old is a **refractive** difference until a cycloplegic refraction and a full correction say otherwise — which is exactly why `opqb-t15-673` lists both among the indicated steps. Amblyopia is also not a *type of squint*, which is what the stem asks for. |\n\n**⚠️ Two notes on this question as printed.** The stem opens \"In the above-mentioned **ease**\" — a typo for *case* — and its antecedent is **Q673**, the schoolboy, read and confirmed rather than assumed. The word \"mutually\" in the printed stem (\"can fix with both eyes mutually\") is the bank's way of saying *either eye, alternately*; it is rendered here as \"with either eye\".\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A squint in which the patient can hold fixation with either eye is alternating, and because neither eye is continuously suppressed the two acuities stay close together.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.106 (Q674), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-675', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'In the same **6-year-old schoolboy** — **6/18 right, 6/12 left**, an alternating convergent squint of **25 degrees** — the **angle of deviation disappeared completely once he wore his correcting glasses**. His refractive error was most probably:',
    options:['Bilateral high myopia.','Bilateral myopic astigmatism.','Unilateral hyperopia.','Bilateral hyperopia.','Anisometropla'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**A squint that vanishes in glasses is accommodative, and accommodative esotropia is driven by hypermetropia in both eyes.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.199 sets out the chain in four steps: the child has **high hypermetropia** → uncorrected, he must **accommodate constantly** to see clearly → accommodation is one leg of the **near-reflex triad (accommodation, convergence, miosis)** → the excess convergence produces the **esotropia**. Put the full correction in front of him and the accommodative demand disappears, taking the convergence — and therefore the deviation — with it. `L29) Sqint I` slide 30 photographs precisely this result: the same child esotropic without spectacles and **both eyes straight** in thick plus lenses.\n\n**Why BILATERAL.** Both eyes are reduced, and by similar amounts — 6/18 and 6/12. A refractive error large enough to blur both eyes to that degree is present in both. And the mechanism requires it: the accommodative effort is a **single, shared** command sent to both eyes, so a bilateral hypermetropia is what generates the constant excess. `L29` slide 29 adds the lecture's own two conditions — **hypermetropia** and a **high AC/A** ratio, at **2.5–5 years** of age.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Bilateral high myopia** | Wrong sign. A myope does **not** need to accommodate for distance — if anything he accommodates less — so myopia produces no excess convergence. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 associates myopia with the opposite deviation: glasses \"may decrease the frequency of deviation in a myopic individual with **exotropia**\". |\n| **Bilateral myopic astigmatism** | The same objection: still myopic, still no accommodative drive to converge. The astigmatism blurs but does not turn the eyes in. |\n| **Unilateral hyperopia** | Anisometropic hypermetropia is a classical cause of **amblyopia** in the affected eye, but it does not drive a symmetrical accommodative esotropia, and it does not fit two nearly equal acuities. Correcting one eye would not abolish a 25° convergence. |\n| **Anisometropla** *(printed so, for anisometropia)* | Unequal refraction between the two eyes. It fails on the same two grounds — the acuities here are close, and unequal error causes amblyopia rather than accommodative convergence. |\n\n**⚠️ What \"disappeared completely\" tells you about the management.** It makes this a **fully** accommodative esotropia, and the treatment is then the glasses and nothing else — no surgery. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201's tutorial-marked box reserves surgery for a constant deviation **\"not corrected by glasses\"**, so an angle that has gone has removed the indication. Had part of the 25° remained in glasses, that residue would have been the partially accommodative component and the surgical target — which is why re-measuring the angle after correction was on Q673's list of indicated steps.\n\n**⚠️ Antecedent note.** This stem's \"above mentioned case\" is **Q673**, the schoolboy himself, not Q674 — Q674 only adds the cover-test finding. The antecedent was read, not assumed. This question also prints **five** options.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'An esotropia that disappears entirely with spectacles is a fully accommodative esotropia, driven by uncorrected bilateral hypermetropia through the accommodation–convergence link, and it needs no surgery.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.106 (Q675), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-676', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'A **child of 4** presented with a **right convergent squint**. On examination the visual acuity was **6/60 in the right eye and 6/6 in the left**. The **angle of deviation was 45 degrees**. The right eye showed a **faint superficial corneal scar**, and the fundus was normal in both eyes. The **cover test** is expected to reveal:',
    options:['Left concomitant convergent squint.','Alternating concomitant convergent squint.','Right concomitant convergent squint.','Alternating esophoria.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**An eye that sees 6/60 cannot take up fixation, so the squint can only be unilateral — and it is the right eye.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.203 gives the reading: on the **cover test** you cover the fixating eye and watch the other; on the **cover–uncover** you watch the covered eye after removing the cover, and *\"Move to fixate again: **Unilateral squint** · Remains deviated: **Alternating squint**.\"* Alternation requires two eyes capable of fixing. Here the right eye is at **6/60** against **6/6** — it will never be the fixing eye, so the left eye fixes permanently and the right stays turned in. That is a **right** squint.\n\n**Where the poor vision came from, and why the stem mentions the scar.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.200: **\"Sensory strabismus: When vision is poor in one eye for a long time this eye may deviate causing sensory exotropia or esotropia.\"** The faint superficial corneal scar is the cause of the poor vision, and the poor vision is the cause of the deviation. p.202's inspection list names exactly what to look for — **\"Examination anterior segment: corneal opacity, cataract\"** — and the normal fundus in both eyes discharges the other half of the same list, optic atrophy and retinoblastoma.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Left concomitant convergent squint** | Names the wrong eye. The left is the **good, fixing** eye; it is the right that deviates. |\n| **Alternating concomitant convergent squint** | Impossible with 6/60 in one eye — the amblyopic eye cannot alternate into fixation. This is exactly the contrast with `opqb-t15-674`, where two nearly equal acuities (6/18, 6/12) made alternation the answer. **The acuities are what separates the two cases**, not the angle and not the age. |\n| **Alternating esophoria** | Wrong on two counts. A **-phoria** is latent, appearing only when fusion is broken (p.198) — this child has a **manifest** 45° deviation visible with both eyes open, which p.199 calls a *tropia*. And \"alternating\" fails for the reason above. |\n\n**⚠️ The scar also changes the prognosis, not just the diagnosis.** This is a **stimulus-deprivation** amblyopia rather than a purely strabismic one, and it is the type with the worst outlook, because the image was degraded from the start rather than merely suppressed *(the four-way classification of amblyopia is not in the course material; only \"strabismic amblyopia\" is named, `ophthalmology.pdf` p.198)*. `opqb-t15-677` takes the case forward and decides what to do about it.\n\n**⚠️ Note.** This question opens a **new case**; it is not a continuation of Q673's schoolboy, and reading it as one would put the wrong acuities on the wrong child.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A squint in an eye seeing 6/60 against 6/6 in the fellow eye must be unilateral, because the amblyopic eye can never take up fixation — here a right concomitant convergent squint, sensory in origin.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.107 (Q676), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-677', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'In the **4-year-old with a right convergent squint** described in the previous question — **6/60 right, 6/6 left, angle 45 degrees, a faint right corneal scar** — the best management for this child is:',
    options:['Occlusion of the right eye.','Occlusion of the left eye.','Squint surgery.','Arrangement for right corneal surgery.'],
    answer:1,
    explanation:"**Right eye is amblyopic, so first step = occlude the better eye (left).**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim from the key page. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The rule the box states in one line.** Occlusion always covers the **sound** eye, to force the amblyopic one to work. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 states the aim it serves — *\"the most effective way to support fusion (binocular vision) is to **treat the amblyopia and equalize the vision**\"* — and names the treatment as **\"patching (to correct amblyopia)\"**, a bullet it marks **Tutorial**. `L29) Sqint I` slide 32 is headed \"Occlusion / Treatment of amblyopia\" and photographs both an opaque and a translucent occluder fitted over spectacles. ⚠️ **Neither source anywhere says that it is the good eye that is covered** — that is the single most important operational fact about the treatment, and the box printed here is the only place in the whole bank that supplies it.\n\n**Why now, and not later.** The child is **4**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.197 puts the amblyopia window \"before the age of **8-10 years**\", so there are years of plasticity left — but the deprivation has already cost him 6/60 and every month counts. `opqb-t15-670` is the same child forty years on, when nothing can be done.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Occlusion of the right eye** | The treatment inverted. Patching the **amblyopic** eye leaves the child functioning on his good eye — which he already does — and deepens the amblyopia he came in with. |\n| **Squint surgery** | Not first. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 orders it explicitly: treat the amblyopia and equalise the vision **first**, and surgical correction \"may **still** be necessary\" afterwards — and its tutorial-marked box conditions it on a deviation \"not corrected by glasses\". Operating on a 45° angle in an eye that cannot see fixes the appearance and abandons the vision; and an unaligned amblyopic eye tends to drift again after surgery. |\n| **Arrangement for right corneal surgery** | The scar is **faint** and **superficial**. A graft on a faint superficial opacity in a 4-year-old trades a small, stable optical defect for a lifetime of graft rejection risk, and paediatric keratoplasty carries the worst survival of any age group. If the media were dense enough to block the image, clearing it would come first — that is the general order, *remove the obstacle, correct the refraction, then patch* *(the three-step order is supplied, not taken from the course material)*. Here it is not. |\n\n**What the full plan looks like.** Cycloplegic refraction and full correction → **patch the left eye**, hours graded to age and severity, with regular review because the patched good eye can itself become amblyopic → re-measure the angle once the acuities are as equal as they will get → **then** consider surgery for the residual deviation, for which `ophthalmology.pdf` p.201 accepts the cosmetic argument in its own words: even where binocular vision may not be achievable, \"the impact of disfiguring strabismus on a patient's self-image is a valid indication for surgery\".",
    objective:'In a unilateral squint with an amblyopic eye the first treatment is occlusion of the BETTER eye to force use of the amblyopic one; surgery and any corneal procedure come after the vision has been equalised.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.107 (Q677), key + explanation p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-678', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'The mother of a **16-month-old boy** suspects that he has **crossed eyes**. On examination, the **corneal light reflex appears in the centre of both pupils**. The mother might think so because of the presence of one of the following **congenital anomalies**:',
    options:['Epicanthus.','Ptosis.','Distichiasis','Trichiasis.'],
    answer:0,
    explanation:"**A central, symmetrical corneal light reflex means the eyes really are straight — so the crossing is an illusion, and epicanthal folds are what create it.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.197 defines the entity: **pseudostrabismus** \"describes the situation when the eyes **falsely appear deviated** but — unlike true strabismus — the **visual axes of both eyes are properly aligned**. Examples include **prominent epicanthal folds** and **hypertelorism**.\" p.202's inspection list repeats it — \"Epicanthal fold or wide nasal bridge (*pseudostabismus*)\" *(printed so, missing an r)* — and its figure panel supplies the pairing the prose leaves out: **EPICANTHUS → PSEUDO ESOTROPIA**, **HYPERTELORISM → PSEUDO EXOTROPIA**.\n\n**Why the reflex settles it.** p.202: normally the light \"is reflected on each cornea **symmetrically**, relative to the pupil and visual axis of each eye\", and \"*in a deviating eye*, the light reflection will be **eccentric** and in a direction **opposite** to that of the deviation.\" The book then gives the test its job in three words: **\"Exclude pseudo-strabismus.\"** A central reflex in both eyes therefore excludes a true deviation at the bedside, with a penlight and no cooperation beyond looking at the light — which is why it comes first in a 16-month-old.\n\n**Why an epicanthal fold makes straight eyes look crossed.** The fold covers the **nasal** sclera of each eye, so white sclera is visible only on the **temporal** side. The brain reads the asymmetry of visible white as inward deviation. `L29) Sqint I` slide 16 proves it in two photographs of one child and no words at all: at rest the child looks convergent; with an adult's fingers **pinching the nasal bridge skin upward** to lift the folds off the inner canthi, the nasal sclera is exposed and **both eyes are seen to be perfectly straight**. Slide 15 pairs it with the opposite illusion — a broad, flat nasal bridge exposing nasal sclera and producing an apparent **divergent** squint.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | What it is, and why it does not do this |\n|---|---|\n| **Ptosis** | A **drooping upper lid**. It makes an eye look small or closed and can occlude the pupil, but it does not shift the apparent position of the iris within the fissure, so it does not mimic a horizontal deviation. |\n| **Distichiasis** | An **extra row of lashes** arising from the meibomian gland orifices. A lash disorder — it irritates the cornea; it changes nothing about apparent alignment. |\n| **Trichiasis** | **Misdirected lashes** rubbing the globe. Again a lash disorder, and in most cases acquired rather than congenital, which the stem's wording also excludes. |\n\n**⚠️ What must still be done.** Pseudostrabismus is a diagnosis of exclusion, and a child can have **both** — epicanthal folds *and* a real squint underneath. If the folds are prominent, if the mother is insistent, or if there is any asymmetry of the reflex, the child still needs the full p.202 sequence: acuity, cycloplegic refraction, anterior segment and **fundus**. Epicanthal folds also flatten with facial growth, so the appearance usually resolves without treatment; where it does not, correction is **cosmetic only** (`opqb-t5-200`).\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'A central, symmetrical corneal light reflex excludes a true deviation, so apparently crossed eyes in an infant with that finding are pseudostrabismus — classically from prominent epicanthal folds hiding the nasal sclera.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.107 (Q678), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-679', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'In a **left sixth nerve paralysis**, the patient can avoid diplopia by which of the following **compensatory mechanisms**?',
    options:['Tilting his head towards the right shoulder.','Turning his face to the right.','Turning his face to the left.','Tilting his head towards the left shoulder.'],
    answer:2,
    explanation:"**The head moves in the direction of action of the paralysed muscle — and the left lateral rectus acts to the left.**\n\n`L30) Squint II` slides 7, 8 and 9 print the rule as a cyan sub-heading repeated under each of the three postures, and it is the deck's only statement of it: **\"Abnormal Head posture: (direction of action of paralysed muscle)\"**, with the three components listed as **1. Face turn (Rt or Lt) · 2. Chin (elevation or depression) · 3. Head tilt (Rt or Lt)**. `ophthalmology.pdf` p.184 then applies it to this nerve without needing to be worked out: a sixth nerve palsy shows an abnormal head position — **\"face turn to the SAME side as the lesion.\"**\n\n**Why it works.** The left lateral rectus abducts the left eye, i.e. moves it **leftward**; that is its field of action, and it is where the eyes cannot move together, so it is where the diplopia is worst (`ophthalmology.pdf` p.184: horizontal diplopia \"increasing in **ipsilateral** gaze\"). By **turning the face to the left**, the patient moves his head into that field so that his **eyes can stay in the primary position** relative to the orbit, where the deviation is smallest. The head does the work the weak muscle cannot.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Which palsy it belongs to |\n|---|---|\n| **Turning his face to the right** | A **right** sixth nerve palsy. The same rule, the other side — this is the mirror distractor, and the only way to fall for it is to reason \"turn away from the weak muscle\", which is exactly backwards. |\n| **Tilting his head towards the right shoulder** | A head **tilt** compensates a **vertical/torsional** palsy, i.e. the **fourth** nerve — `ophthalmology.pdf` p.183, and `opqb-t15-680` asks it directly. A sixth nerve palsy is purely horizontal, so a tilt does nothing for it. |\n| **Tilting his head towards the left shoulder** | Same objection; and for a *fourth* nerve palsy the tilt goes to the **opposite** side, so even as a fourth-nerve answer it would need a right-sided lesion. |\n\n**The three postures, matched to the three nerves** *(the deck states the rule and never works an example; the pairings below come from `ophthalmology.pdf` ch.14)*:\n\n| Palsy | Deviation | Head posture |\n|---|---|---|\n| **VI** (lateral rectus) | **esotropia**, abduction lost | **face turn TOWARD** the lesion (p.184) |\n| **IV** (superior oblique) | **hypertropia**, worst in adduction | **head tilt AWAY** from the lesion, plus chin depression (p.183) |\n| **III** | \"down and out\", ptosis | none needed — the **ptosis masks the diplopia** (p.182) |\n\n**⚠️ Why the head posture is worth finding.** In a child it may be the only complaint: `ophthalmology.pdf` p.200 records that a **congenital fourth nerve palsy is the commonest cause of head tilt in children**, and \"a child with head tilt should be referred to the ophthalmologist to exclude superior oblique palsy\". A posture adopted to preserve binocular vision is a motility sign, not an orthopaedic one.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'An abnormal head posture moves in the direction of action of the paralysed muscle, so a left sixth nerve palsy is compensated by a face turn to the left — toward the side of the lesion.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.107 (Q679), key p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-680', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-neuro-motil',
    stem:'In a **right fourth nerve paralysis**, the patient can avoid diplopia by which of the following **compensatory mechanisms**?',
    options:['Turning his face to the left.','Turning his face to the right.','Tilting his head to the right shoulder.','Tilting his head to the left shoulder'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"Superior oblique palsy\n\n→ vertical diplopia worse on looking down and in\n\nCompensation: **tilt head to opposite shoulder**\n\n---\n\n*(The box above is the bank's own printed explanation, transcribed verbatim from the key page. Its second line stops mid-sentence, as printed. The notes below are added for study.)*\n\n**The fourth nerve supplies one muscle, and the compensation is a tilt.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.183 lists the features: **hypertropia** in the primary position, increasing **in adduction**; weak depression, especially in adduction; **vertical diplopia** increasing in **downgaze**; and an abnormal head position — **chin depression, plus face turn and head tilt to the OPPOSITE (normal) side**. Its own box gives the muscle's actions: **\"Function of SO4: abduction, internal rotation, depression.\"** A **right** fourth nerve palsy is therefore compensated by tilting to the **LEFT** shoulder.\n\n**Why the tilt is away from the palsy** *(the reason is not in the book — supplied)*. Tilting the head to one side triggers the **ocular counter-roll**: the eye on the lower side must **intort** to keep the world upright. Intorsion is the superior oblique's primary action (`L1,2` slide 69: **\"Superior Oblique | Intorsion | Depression | Abduction\"**), so tilting **toward** the palsied side asks the paralysed muscle for exactly the movement it cannot make, and the vertical deviation and the diplopia get worse. Tilting **away** asks the intact fellow eye to do the work instead. That is also the principle behind the **Bielschowsky head-tilt test** — deliberately tilting the head toward the suspect side to unmask the hypertropia — ⚠️ which `L30) Squint II`'s own record confirms is **not named anywhere in the deck**, even though slides 9 and 14 photograph spontaneous compensatory tilts.\n\n**Why each distractor is wrong:**\n\n| Option | Why not |\n|---|---|\n| **Tilting his head to the right shoulder** | The correct **manoeuvre**, the wrong **side** — and the one that makes the patient worse, for the reason above. It is the single most instructive distractor here. |\n| **Turning his face to the left** | A **face turn** compensates a **horizontal** palsy, i.e. the sixth nerve (`opqb-t15-679`). A fourth nerve palsy's deviation is vertical and torsional; turning the face does not address it. |\n| **Turning his face to the right** | The same objection. |\n\n**What else the fourth nerve palsy brings.** `ophthalmology.pdf` p.183 gives its aetiology as **microvascular infarction** (diabetes, hypertension), **trauma** — which can produce a **bilateral** palsy — **compressive** lesions, and rarely **congenital**. p.200 adds the paediatric point: a **congenital** fourth nerve palsy is the **commonest cause of head tilt in children**, and a child with a head tilt should be referred to exclude superior oblique palsy. And p.201 sets the timing for any of the three palsies: in trauma or diabetes, **spontaneous resolution may occur within 6 months**; `L30` slide 16 makes the same figure the surgical threshold — \"After 6 months\", then resection–recession for an incomplete paralysis and muscle transposition for a complete one.",
    objective:'A fourth nerve palsy weakens intorsion and depression, so the head tilts to the OPPOSITE shoulder — tilting toward the palsy demands the intorsion the superior oblique can no longer supply.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.107 (Q680), key + explanation p.108' },

  { id:'opqb-t15-681', bank:'gradegain', module:'ophtho', chapter:'op-squint',
    stem:'The **most important procedure** in the examination of a **child with a unilateral squint** is:',
    options:['Visual acuity.','Red reflex.','Refraction.','Fundus examination'],
    answer:3,
    explanation:"**Because a unilateral squint in a child can be the first sign of a retinoblastoma, and the fundus is where that is found.**\n\n`ophthalmology.pdf` p.202's assessment of a case of squint ends its work-up column with one line: **\"Fundus examination: optic atrophy, retinoblastoma.\"** It is the only item in the whole chapter that is both **sight-threatening and life-threatening**, and the mechanism that connects it to the squint is on p.200 — **sensory strabismus**: *\"When vision is poor in one eye for a long time this eye may deviate.\"* A tumour blinds the eye, and the blind eye turns. The turn is what the parents notice; the tumour is what kills.\n\n**⚠️ Why \"unilateral\" is doing the work in the stem.** An **alternating** squint means both eyes can fix, so both eyes see — reassuring. A **unilateral** squint means one eye has been abandoned, and the question is always *why that eye*. The answer may be benign (strabismic amblyopia) or it may be a cataract, a corneal opacity, optic atrophy or a retinoblastoma. The fundus discriminates.\n\n**⚠️ The tag on the page.** This whole examination sequence sits inside the two pages the book marks **\"The next two pages are from tutorial .. Skip in Final\"**. Recorded as data about what the book says; the bank asks it repeatedly, and the fundus line is the most consequential sentence inside the skipped block.\n\n**Why the other three are ranked below it** — and all three are genuinely part of the work-up, which is what makes this question a ranking rather than an elimination:\n\n| Option | Where it sits |\n|---|---|\n| **Visual acuity** | **The strongest rival, and defensible.** p.202 puts it first: \"Visual acuity (amblyopia ??)\". It establishes *that* the eye sees badly. But it does not establish *why*, and in a pre-verbal or barely verbal child it is the hardest of the four to obtain — `opqb-t1-29` keys **CSM testing** for a 1-year-old for exactly that reason. |\n| **Red reflex** | A **screening** step, and a good one — `opmcq-c1-35` matches each abnormal colour to its disease, retinoblastoma to a **white** reflex. But it is a coarse test: a small posterior tumour, an optic atrophy or a macular scar can leave the red reflex normal. It raises the alarm; it cannot clear the eye. |\n| **Refraction** | Essential to **treatment** — the cycloplegic refraction that reveals the hypermetropia driving an accommodative esotropia (`opqb-t15-671`) — and it must be done. But it is a step in management, not the step that excludes the dangerous cause. |\n\n**Clinical bottom line.** Do all four. But if only one examination could be performed on a child with a one-sided squint, it is the **dilated fundus examination**, because it is the only one that can turn a cosmetic complaint into a diagnosis that has to be acted on this week.\n\nWritten for this bank — ophthalmology qb.pdf prints no explanation here.",
    objective:'Every child with a unilateral squint needs a fundus examination, because the deviating eye may be blind from optic atrophy or a retinoblastoma — the only sight- and life-threatening cause in the work-up.',
    source:'ophthalmology qb.pdf p.107 (Q681), key p.108' }
